d there is no repo code to pin it.", "Whether the python 3.14 / no-wheels constraint reflects the eventual execution environment; if it does, model sizes must shrink substantially.", "Whether pre-existing checkpoints with optimizer state might become available (none exist now); that would remove the need for the training arm entirely."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-bd54878f-a11f-47d0-b6f5-5c9bc095e073", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "end_researcher_turn: {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## 1) Inventory (what actually exists)\n\n**Repository `/workspace`: empty except for the task file.**\n- `find /workspace -maxdepth 2` \u2192 only `task.json` (699 B). No subdirectories.\n- **No BAdam code**, no block-coordinate-descent optimizer, no `badam`/`BAdam` file anywhere on the filesystem (`find / -xdev -iname '*badam*'` \u2192 0 hits).\n- **No Adam/AdamW baseline implementation** (no torch, no hand-rolled optimizer module).\n- **No finetuning scripts** (`find / -iname '*finetune*'` \u2192 0 hits), no training entrypoints, no configs, no YAML/JSON model specs, no data files (`datasets: []` in task.json).\n- **No SVD / spectral-analysis utilities**, no ESD or power-law fitting code.\n- **No checkpoints, logs, or weight/update artifacts** (`find / -xdev` for `*.safetensors`, `*.pt`, `*.ckpt` \u2192 0 hits); no `~/.cache`, no HF hub cache, no home-directory state at all.\n- `task.json` also carries `\"documents\": {\"track.md\": \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"}` \u2014 i.e. the harness itself declares this a specification-only deliverable.\n\n**Environment.**\n- `python3` = **3.14.4** (GCC 15.2.0). Very new interpreter \u2192 wheel-availability risk for any pinned reproduction env.\n- Project deps **not preinstalled**: `numpy`, `scipy`, `torch`, `transformers`, `peft`, `datasets`, `accelerate`, `matplotlib`, `pandas`, `sklearn`, `powerlaw` all raise `ModuleNotFoundError`. Only system/OS packages exist (ansible, apt tooling, Jinja2, `PyYAML 6.0.3`, Pillow, etc.). `pip 25.1.1` present but **pip cache empty** and **network is blocked** (live probe: `URLError -3 Temporary failure in name resolution`), so nothing can be installed.\n- **No GPU**: `nvidia-smi` fails (\"couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver\"). `gpu_enabled: false`.\n- Generous CPU/RAM: `nproc = 224`, ~2 TB RAM (2011 GB total, ~1.9 TB available) \u2014 relevant only to a *hypothetical* run environment, not to this one.\n\n**What this means.** The deliverable can only be a **design specification** (metrics, slicing, sampling, protocol, decision rule, analysis-code contract) plus feasibility notes. Nothing in this sandbox can run a finetuning run or even a `numpy` SVD; writing a from-scratch stdlib SVD would only ever validate my own metric plumbing on synthetic data, never the scientific claim, so I will not present any such thing as a result. I have executed no experiment and claim no outcome.\n\n---\n\n## 2) Proposed approach (design level, not the final design)\n\n**2.1 Define the \"learning perturbation\" \u2014 three nested quantities, all per 2-D parameter tensor.**\n- **P1 (primary): accumulated weight displacement** `\u0394W_t = W_t \u2212 W_0` per module, stored in fp32/fp16\u2192fp32. This is the actual perturbation the optimizer induces on the pretrained model, and it is the quantity whose heavy tails are claimed in the ESD-generalization literature (Martin\u2013Mahoney style).\n- **P2 (secondary): interval update matrix** `U_{[t1,t2]} = W_{t2} \u2212 W_{t1}` \u2014 isolates what the optimizer is doing *now*, so early-training behavior is not washed out by accumulation.\n- **P3 (diagnostic): optimizer-step matrix** captured by instrumenting the step (`delta = p_before \u2212 p_after`) and summing it over short windows, per optimizer. Needed because BAdam's mechanism is *temporal sparsity of updates* (one block active per step), which P1 integrates away.\n- Reference/control spectrum: `W_0` itself (pretrained ESD is known to be heavy-tailed; it is the baseline the perturbation must be compared against, and a scaling confound).\n- **Explicit design prediction to test:** BAdam's blocking is over *parameter groups/tensors*, not a low-rank factorization inside a matrix. So for **full-parameter** BAdam, per-matrix `\u0394W` is still a sum of full-support Adam-style steps, just accumulated at ~1/B of the steps with stale second moments \u2192 spectra should be *near*-comparable to Adam with a modest effective-rank deficit that shrinks with training. That prediction is falsifiable and distinguishes the mechanism from \"BAdam is low-rank\". If the question is instead about **BAdam applied to LoRA** (the B-AdamW / \"BAdam for LoRA\" setting), block structure *is* low-rank by construction and the answer is different a priori \u2014 this is the single biggest fork (see \u00a73).\n\n**2.2 Module slicing.** Analyze only 2-D tensors; every slice reported per (model, optimizer, seed, step, module-type, layer-index):\n- Attention: `q_proj`, `k_proj`, `v_proj`, `o_proj` (and, for GQA/MQA, per-head reshaped `k`/`v` spectra, stacked).\n- MLP: `gate_proj`, `up_proj`, `down_proj` (SwiGLU) or `fc1`/`fc2`.\n- Embedding matrix and LM head separately (note tied/untied); these are the wide, highly anisotropic ones.\n- Layer bins: early / middle / late (and per-layer curves) to expose depth dependence \u2014 the interesting claim in the BAdam literature is that the last blocks/unembedding move most.\n- Norm gains / biases / 1-D params: **excluded from spectral claims** (no well-defined effective rank), reported separately as displacement norm and coordinate-wise dispersion so the \"across modules\" claim is honestly scoped.\n\n**2.3 Spectral descriptors (all scale-invariant, plus scale reported as covariate).**\n- **Heavy-tail exponent**: MLE power-law exponent \u03b1 on *squared* singular values in the tail, with `x_min` chosen by KS (Clauset\u2013Shalizi\u2013Newman style), bootstrap CI, **plus** a log-binned least-squares slope and a two-regime (\"bulk slope / head slope\", bimodal-slope) fit. Always reported against **competing nulls**: log-normal, exponential, and truncated power law (AIC / Vuong-type comparison).\n- **Tail mass**: fraction of Frobenius energy (\u03a3\u03c3\u00b2) above a bulk-edge threshold defined from a Marchenko\u2013Pastur surrogate; also top-k energy fraction for k \u2208 {1, 4, 16, 64}.\n- **Effective rank**: Roy\u2013Vetterli `r_eff = exp(H(\u03c3/\u2016\u03c3\u2016\u2081))`, and **participation ratio** `PR = (\u03a3\u03c3\u00b2)\u00b2 / \u03a3\u03c3\u2074` (both reported; they disagree in exactly the regime that matters).\n- **Stable rank** `\u2016W\u2016_F\u00b2 / \u2016W\u2016\u2082\u00b2`; **spectral entropy** of normalized eigenvalues and its normalized version; **max/mean eigenvalue ratio** and **bulk-to-edge ratio**; condition number.\n- **Confounders logged per module**: `\u2016\u0394W\u2016_F/\u2016W_0\u2016_F`, per-tensor step count actually applied (BAdam sees ~1/B of steps), number of distinct updates accumulated, matrix dims.\n\n**2.4 Checkpoint / step sampling.** Save `W_0` (immutable snapshot) plus `W_t` at a hybrid grid: every step for the first ~50, then log-spaced and linear-spaced union at \u2248 1%, 2%, 5%, 10%, 20%, 35%, 50%, 70%, 85%, 100% of the step budget, plus best-checkpoint and final. Store P3 window-sums continuously (cheap, fp32, per module) so dense update-spectrum curves come from P3 while expensive full SVDs run only on the ~10\u201315 saved checkpoints. Storage: one `.safetensors`/`.npz` shard per (step \u00d7 module-type) with a manifest; full SVD via `torch.svd_lowrank`/randomized SVD for top-k plus exact `numpy.linalg.svd` on CPU fp32 for full spectra (feasible at 1\u20137B widths on the stated 224-CPU host).\n\n**2.5 Comparison protocol vs Adam.**\n- Same base model, same data, same **fixed example order and seed**, same batch size, same warmup, same weight 