, empty pip cache → **no install path**. **No GPU** (`nvidia-smi` fails).\n- Consequence: the optimizer in §2 is **specified by me in pinned pseudo-code**, not imported from a repo, and every blocking parameter is an explicit design assumption.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Rationale: what the design must actually discriminate\n\nThree separable claims are tangled in the question, and the design keeps them separate:\n\n- **(C1) Heavy tail** of the perturbation spectrum — a shape property of one matrix's singular values, confounded by the pretrained spectrum (§5).\n- **(C2) Effective-rank comparability** — a paired, cross-optimizer equivalence statement on the *same* starting weights.\n- **(C3) Mechanism attribution** — BAdam's distinguishing feature is **temporal sparsity of updates**: one block of parameters is updated per step, so each tensor receives ≈ `T/B` applied updates instead of `T`, with Adam moments that go stale between activations. The block structure of BAdam is over **parameter groups**, not a factorization inside a matrix. Therefore the design's load-bearing control is a **step-thinned AdamW** arm (full-model update every `B` steps): without it, any effective-rank deficit is unattributable.\n\nThe mechanism (C3) lives in the *step* matrices (P3); the claim in the question is about the *accumulated* perturbation (P1). §9 states in advance which verdicts come apart if P1 and P3 disagree.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Pinned optimizer specification (assumption-pinned, not repo-pinned)\n\n```\n# BAdam-full — pinned specification. Every constant is an assumption (A1..A9),\n# NOT a property of any canonical implementation (no code exists in /workspace).\ninit: W_0 = pretrained weights; AdamW state (m,v) allocated ONLY for the active block\nblocks: split params into ordered list [Bl_1 .. Bl_B]          # A1 granularity\n    Bl_U = {embed_tokens, lm_head(unembedding), all 1-D params}  # A5 unembedding handling\n    Bl_l = {q,k,v,o,gate,up,down of layer l} for l = L-1 .. 0    # A2 order: reverse/top-down\n           (+ layer-norm gains of layer l)                        # A6 norms ride their layer block\nfor t = 1..T:                                                   # T = matched step count\n    if t <= WARMUP:  active = ALL blocks; B_active = full model  # A3 warmup\n    else:            active = Bl_[ ((t-WARMUP-1) mod (B-1)) + 2 ] # cycle excludes Bl_U re-pinning\n                     # cycle order: layer L-1, L-2, ..., 0, then Bl_U once per cycle-end\n    g = backward(full batch)          # FULL fwd+bwd every step: gradients for all params\n    for p in active: p, m_p, v_p = adamw_step(p, g[p], m_p, v_p, lr_t, b1=0.9, b2=0.999,\n                                              eps=1e-8, weight_decay=0.0)   # A4 base optimizer\n    # inactive p: untouched (m_p, v_p not allocated / not updated) -> A7 no implicit momentum decay\n    lr_t = cosine decay with linear warmup over 3% of T          # A8 schedule (matched across arms)\nclip: global grad-norm 1.0, computed over the FULL gradient (identical across all arms)\n```\n\n| # | Blocking parameter | Pinned value | Alternatives (ablation tier) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A1 | granularity | per transformer layer + one unembedding block | per-tensor; module-group (attn/MLP); random partition; whole-model (≡ AdamW) |\n| A2 | ordering | reverse topological (last layer first), cyclic | forward; random order |\n| A3 | warmup | 10 full-model steps | 0; `B` steps |\n| A4 | base optimizer inside block | AdamW β=(0.9,0.999), ε=1e-8, wd=0.0 | wd=0.1 |\n| A5 | unembedding handling | its own block, visited once per cycle | excluded entirely; merged with layer L-1 |\n| A6 | norm/1-D params | attached to their layer's block | own blocks |\n| A7 | stale-state policy | frozen between activations (no decay, no refresh) | refresh on activation |\n| A8 | LR/schedule | cosine, 3% warmup; best of {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5} per arm | fixed shared LR |\n| A9 | STEPS_PER_BLOCK | 1 | 2; 4 |\n\nFor Model A (`L=16`): `B = 17` blocks. With `T ≈ 1560` steps (§11), **each tensor receives ≈ 92 applied updates** under BAdam vs 1560 under AdamW — a 17× difference in applied-update count, which is exactly what control arm 4 exists to isolate.\n\n**Compute-honesty note (stated in the write-up):** BAdam performs a full forward+backward every step and discards ≈ `(B−1)/B` of the computed gradients. It saves optimizer memory, not FLOPs. Matched-step comparison is therefore FLOP-honest, and any BAdam disadvantage is *not* a compute-budget artifact.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The three perturbation objects (per 2-D parameter tensor, per run/seed)\n\n| id | definition | role | retention |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **P1** | `ΔW = W_t − W_0` | **primary / headline** — the perturbation the question asks about | full matrix at 3 checkpoints; spectra + top-64 singular vectors at all 12 grid points |\n| **P2** | `U_[t1,t2] = W_t2 − W_t1` | interval view; prevents early dynamics being washed out by accumulation | derived from P1 snapshots at adjacent grid points |\n| **P3** | `S_w = Σ_{t∈window} (p_before − p_after)` | **explanatory** — direct view of temporal sparsity | 48 windows/run; spectra computed inline at window boundary, matrices discarded |\n| **W₀** | pretrained weights | reference spectrum + confound control (§5) | stored **once per model**, shared by all runs/arms/seeds |\n\nAll stored in fp32; gradients in bf16; a single fp32 master copy per parameter in every arm so that precision differences cannot masquerade as spectral differences.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Module slicing (counts computed from configs)\n\nOnly **2-D** tensors enter spectral claims. Verified counts:\n\n| Model | hidden `h` | `L` | inter | vocab | GQA | 2-D tensors | exponent-qualified (`min-dim ≥ 1024`) | rank-only (`32 ≤ min-dim < 1024`) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **A** Llama-3.2-1B-class (SwiGLU 4×, tied) | 2048 | 16 | 8192 | 128 256 | 32q/8kv | 113 | **81** | 32 (all `k`/`v`, 512×2048) |\n| **B** Qwen2.5-1.5B-class (SwiGLU 5.83×, tied) | 1536 | 28 | 8960 | 151 936 | 12q/2kv | 197 | **141** | 56 (all `k`/`v`, 128×1536) |\n| **C** Pythia-410M-class (MHA, fused QKV, untied head) — *conditional* | 1024 | 24 | 4096 | 50 304 | 16/16 | 122 | **122** | 0 |\n\nReporting bins (every table is crossed with these): **module type** (`q,k,v,o,gate,up,down,embed,lm_head`), **depth bin** (early / middle / late, and per-layer curves), **model**, **arm**, **seed**, **grid point**, **object** (P1/P2/P3).\n\nRules carried forward unchanged:\n- **GQA `k`/`v`**: never pooled as one rectangular matrix. Reshape to **per-head** `[64 × h]` and analyse as stacked per-head spectra (A: 8 heads × 16 layers × {k,v} = 256 per-head matrices; B: 2 × 28 × 2 = 112), plus a reported \"stacked\" descriptor for the unreshaped tensor labelled as such. `min-dim = 64` ⇒ per-head results are **rank-descriptors only, never exponents**.\n- **Fused QKV (Model C)**: split along rows into q/k/v thirds before analysis; record the split in the manifest so descriptors are reproducible.\n- **Embeddings / LM head**: analysed (they are the widest matrices and the BAdam unembedding block) but reported as a separate bin, never pooled into the per-layer aggregate.\n- **Norm gains, biases, 1-D params**: **descriptive only** — no effective rank is claimed. Report `‖Δθ‖₂/‖θ₀‖₂`, coordinate-wise dispersion, and Gini of |Δθ|. The headline statement is explicitly scoped to \"2-D projection modules\".\n- **Sample-rank-deficient flag**: if `PR < 8` or `rank(ΔW) < 8`, emit `RANK_DEFICIENT`, report rank fraction, and suppress exponent and effective-rank-ratio claims for that cell.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The W₀ confound (not a footnote)\n\nPretrained spectra are themselves heavy-tailed, so a heavy-tailed `ΔW` could mean either \"the optimizer creates heavy-tailed structure\" or \"a heavy-tailed matrix was moved\". Controls, all pre-registered:\n\n1. **`W₀` is shared across arms.** The paired P1 comparison (BAdam vs AdamW on the same `W₀`, same seed, same data order) cancels most of the confound by construction. Report **both** the paired difference (confound-cancelled) **and** absolute descriptor values (confound-present), and never draw the conclusion from the absolute column alone.\n2. **Alignment test — \"is the tail of ΔW inside W₀'s top subspace?\"** For each module: principal angles between the top-`k=64` left/right singular subspaces of `ΔW` and of `W₀`; report the overlap score against a **random-rotation null** with matched spectrum, and the off-diagonal energy fraction of `U₀ᵀ ΔW V₀`. High overlap ⇒ the tail is inherited geometry, not optimizer-created structure. Low overlap ⇒ intrinsic.\n3. **Inheritance regression.** Across modules within an arm: regress `α(ΔW)` on `α(W₀)`. Report slope and intercept **per arm**; if both arms share slope ≈ 1 and similar intercept, heavy tails are inherited and are not a BAdam-vs-Adam discriminator.\n4. **Displaced-copy null.** `ΔW_null = R₁ S(W₀) R₂ᵀ · ‖ΔW‖_F/‖S(W₀)‖_F` (random rotations of a diagonal with `W₀`'s spectrum). Its descriptors define exactly what \"heavy-tailed because `W₀` is\" looks like; `ΔW` must beat it to support C1.\n5. **`‖ΔW‖_F / ‖W₀‖_F` is a required column in every comparison table**, together with `α(W₀)`, `r_eff(W₀)`, `PR(W₀)`, `‖W₀‖_F`. The norm ratio is also a **covariate** in the mixed model (§8) and a **stratifier** for the per-module band plots, because the descriptor comparison means different things at 0.1% vs 5% relative displacement.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Descriptor set (all computed from `σ₁ ≥ … ≥ σ_p > 0`)\n\nScale-invariant, reported on `s² = σ²` unless noted:\n\n**Effective rank / concentration:** `r_eff = exp(−Σ pᵢ ln pᵢ)` with `pᵢ = σᵢ/Σσ` (Roy–Vetterli); **participation ratio** `PR = (Σσ²)²/Σσ⁴`; **stable rank** `‖W‖_F²/‖W‖₂²`; normalized spectral entropy `H(s²)/ln p`; `max/mean` eigenvalue ratio; bulk-to-edge ratio; condition number. (`r_eff` and `PR` are both reported and both must pass equivalence — they disagree in exactly the regime at issue.)\n\n**Heavy-tail shape:**\n- **α_MLE** — Clauset–Shalizi–Newman MLE power-law exponent on `s²` with **KS-chosen `x_min`**, bootstrap CI (1000 resamples).\n- **α_logbin** — slope of log-binned `s²` CCDF (equal-width bins, 60 bins), OLS with HC standard errors.\n- **Two-regime (\"bimodal slope\") fit** — separate bulk and head slopes plus the crossover index; a single α is known to be an oversimplification here.\n- **Tail mass** — fraction of `Σσ²` above `x_min`; above a Marchenko–Pastur-derived bulk edge; and top-`k` energy for `k ∈ {1,4,16,64}`.\n- **Competing-null fit table** — pure power law vs **log-normal**, **truncated power law**, **exponential**, scored by AIC/BIC with the pre-registered margin (§7 R1).\n\n**Confounders/covariates (in every table):** `‖ΔW‖_F/‖W₀‖_F`, applied-update count for that tensor (`T` vs `≈T/B`), number of distinct applied updates accumulated, matrix dims, `W₀` descriptors, loss and step index, and per-block activation phase (position within BAdam's cycle at the snapshot).\n\n**Spectral nulls (computed, not assumed):** matched-Frobenius-norm Gaussian / Marchenko–Pastur surrogate; `ΔW_null` displaced `W₀` copy (§5.4); **equal-update-count surrogate** = sum of `n_updates` i.i.d. random matrices with matched per-step norm — this separates genuine low-rank structure from mere small-sample rank deficiency, which is the central technical trap in comparing a 92-update tensor to a 1560-update one.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Checkpoint grid and heavy-tail acceptance/rejection rules\n\n**Sampling grid (12 points per run):** dense early `t ∈ {10, 25, 50}`, then fractions of total budget `T` at **1%, 3%, 5%, 10%, 25%, 50%, 75%, 100%**, plus the best-dev-loss checkpoint (may coincide). Full matrices at **{25%, 75%, 100%}**; spectra + top-64 singular vectors at all 12; `W₀` and a data-order hash recorded once per run for verification. P3 uses **48** windows (log-spaced, denser early).\n\n**Heavy-tailed = TRUE for a module only if ALL of R1–R6 pass.** Otherwise the cell is reported as its failure label; failures are counted, never averaged away.\n\n- **R1 fit quality.** Pure power law must beat log-normal, truncated power law and exponential by **ΔAIC ≥ 10** (pre-registered margin) on the tail region. Otherwise `NOT_HEAVY_TAILED(best-null-wins)`.\n- **R2 tail coverage.** The region above `x_min` must contain **≥ 10%** of the `s²` points **and** ≥ 10% of the `s²` mass, with at least 50 points. Concretely: `min-dim = 2048` needs ≥ 205 tail points; `1536` needs ≥ 154. Otherwise `INSUFFICIENT_SUPPORT`.\n- **R3 drift.** If α moves systematically across the grid — Spearman |ρ(α, t)| ≥ 0.8, or total drift > 0.5 — the cell is `DRIFTING`, which **rejects** the heavy-tail claim as stated (a shape that is not stable through finetuning is not a property of the perturbation).\n- **R4 checkpoint instability (researcher-required addition).** If the heavy-tail verdict differs between **any two** grid points for that module, the module is labelled `UNSTABLE`. It is **counted as its own column in the verdict table** and is excluded from the \"heavy-tailed\" numerator. It is never averaged into a fractional score.\n- **R5 rank deficiency.** `PR < 8` or `rank < 8` ⇒ `RANK_DEFICIENT`; descriptors reported, exponent claims suppressed.\n- **R6 degeneracy.** `x_min` above the 90th percentile, fewer than 2 MLE iterations to convergence, or fitted `α ∉ [1.01, 12]` ⇒ `NOT_ASSESSABLE`.\n\n**Comparability criteria (approved, pre-registered, equivalence logic — TOST, not failure-to-reject):**\n- per-module `log r_eff` ratio and `log PR` ratio must have **CIs entirely inside [0.8, 1.25]**;\n- per-module `Δα = α_BAdam − α_AdamW` CI **inside [−0.5, +0.5]**;\n- aggregated as a **rank-weighted** mean log-ratio across qualifying 2-D modules (weight = `min(m,n)`, normalized; sensitivity analyses with equal weights and with depth-balanced weights — if the verdict flips under reweighting, that is reported as verdict-fragile);\n- tested by CI-based TOST plus the two one-sided p-values.\n\n**Mandatory disaggregation (researcher-required):** for each descriptor and module type, report (a) the full per-module distribution of ratios (ECDF + per-layer curves), (b) **counts of modules in-band / above / below by module type**, and (c) a formal **module-type × optimizer interaction test** in the mixed model. An aggregate that passes while attention sits above the band and MLP below is a *different finding* and must be labelled **PARTIAL/HETEROGENEOUS**, overriding the aggregate verdict in the headline.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Comparison arms, alignment and statistics\n\n**Arms (main grid, identical data order/seed/batch/clip/schedule):**\n\n| # | arm | purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **BAdam-full** (§2) | the object of the question |\n| 2 | **AdamW**, own best LR | primary reference (\"Adam\") |\n| 3 | **AdamW at BAdam's LR** | separates mechanism from step size |\n| 4 | **Step-thinned AdamW** — full-model AdamW update only every `B` steps, no block structure | **required mechanism control**: matched applied-update count per tensor (~T/B). Without it, \"BAdam differs\" ≠ \"block scheduling matters\" |\n| 5 | **SGD-momentum**, best LR | negative control: pipeline must detect a known non-comparable optimizer, else the pipeline is under-powered |\n\nLR selection: 3-config preliminary sweep per arm per model, 1 seed, not counted in the main grid, chosen by dev loss before any spectral analysis (chosen-LR recorded).\n\n**Alignment.** (a) **Matched step** — *primary*: same step index, same example order. (b) **Matched train/dev loss** — *secondary*: linear interpolation along each arm's trajectory; **dropped with an explicit note if the loss trajectories never overlap**, and the drop is stated in the results. (c) All headline claims are made at matched step; the loss-matched view is labelled as a secondary lens that may favour BAdam.\n\n**Statistics.** Unit = module `(type, layer)`. Paired across arms by module identity and seed. Mixed model per descriptor: `log-ratio ~ module_type * depth_bin + norm_ratio + applied_updates` with seed random intercept and **cluster bootstrap over layers** (stratified by module type, 10 000 resamples) for CIs. **Primary family** = {`r_eff` ratio, `PR` ratio} on P1 at 100% and 50% for arms 1-vs-2, Holm-corrected, α = 0.05. Everything else secondary/exploratory and labelled as such. Seeds ≥ 3 per cell (5 if affordable); report the observed between-seed spread as a limitation if < 5 — no power numbers are invented here.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Pre-registered verdict table, and where P1 and P3 come apart\n\n**Headline table (one row per model, one column set per conjunct; filled from data, never from expectation):**\n\n| model | heavy-tailed (BAdam) | heavy-tailed (AdamW) | α comparable (CI ⊂ ±0.5) | r_eff comparable (CI ⊂ [0.8,1.25]) | PR comparable | heterogeneity override | R2/R4/R5 failure counts | **headline AND** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A / B | YES/NO/UNSTABLE(n) | … | … | … | … | PARTIAL flag | per module type | YES/NO/INCONCLUSIVE |\n\n**The P1-vs-P3 rule, fixed in advance.** P1 is the headline because the question is about the learning perturbation; P3 is the explanation because the mechanism is temporal sparsity. If they disagree, **the answer is not a plain \"comparable\"**:\n\n| P1 (accumulated) | P3 (step windows) | Pre-declared reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| comparable | comparable | **YES**, with mechanism consistent: block-wise Adam steps integrate to an Adam-like displacement. |\n| comparable | **not** comparable | **Qualified NO on mechanism, YES on endpoint.** Headline must read: *\"the accumulated perturbation is spectrally comparable to Adam's, but the per-window updates are not; comparability is an artifact of accumulation over the cycle, not of equivalent update geometry.\"* A P1-only \"yes\" is explicitly **not** an admissible summary. |\n| **not** comparable | comparable | **NO.** Divergence is generated during *accumulation* (stale moments, interleaving, drift), not in the step itself — this refutes the \"Adam-in-a-block ⇒ same thing, slower\" reading and is the most mechanistically interesting negative. |\n| not comparable | not comparable | **NO**, straightforwardly. |\n\nAdditionally: if **both** arms fail C1 (R1/R2), the heavy-tail conjunct is unsupported for *either* optimizer and the question's premise is reported as not supported, regardless of how good the comparability numbers look.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Secondary arm: LoRA / B-AdamW — separate table, never merged\n\nClearly labelled exploratory. Setup: LoRA `r ∈ {8, 64}` on all linear modules, AdamW vs block-scheduled B-AdamW over per-layer LoRA `A`/`B` tensors. **Stated up front in the paper text: in this arm low rank is constructional** (`Δ(AB)` has rank ≤ 2r), so a low effective rank there is **not evidence about BAdam's mechanism** and cannot enter the main verdict. Rank-aware reformulation: descriptors computed on `Δ(AB)`; report `r_eff / (2r)` (fraction of allocated capacity used), tail mass within the available `2r`, exponent estimated with `p = 2r` noted everywhere (finite-size caveat severe at r=8), and comparisons against a **capacity-matched** reference (`r` vs `2r` LoRA) rather than against full-dimensional nulls.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Ablations ranked by overturning power\n\n| rank | ablation | what it can overturn |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | **Step-thinned AdamW (arm 4)** | The entire mechanism attribution: if arms 1 and 4 are equivalent on all descriptors, any BAdam-vs-AdamW gap is explained by *applied-update count*, not by block scheduling. Highest overturning power; not optional. |\n| **2** | **Block granularity** (per-layer → per-tensor; → module-group; → whole-model) | The claim's dependence on BAdam's defining choice: a verdict that flips with granularity means the conclusion is about *that* schedule, not about block-coordinate descent. |\n| **3** | **Block ordering** (reverse → forward → random) | Whether depth-dependent staleness (last-block-first, then rarely revisited) drives per-layer effective-rank patterns. Can reverse the sign of depth-bin effects. |\n| **4** | **Random-block-partition control** | Whether the bi-structured partition (embeddings/head as its own block, layer-aligned blocks) matters at all, versus any partition of the same coarseness. |\n| **5** | **STEPS_PER_BLOCK {1,2,4}** and **warmup {0,10,B}** | Dose–response in update sparsity; a monotone trend in `r_eff` ratio vs steps-per-block is the cleanest positive mechanism evidence. |\n| 6 | wd 0.0 → 0.1 | Whether tail-shape differences are weight-decay artifacts. |\n\n**Explicit statement required in the write-up:** the main conclusion rests on A1 (granularity), A9 (steps-per-block), A8 (LR selection) and on arm 4. A3/A5–A7 are secondary; if any of ablations 1–4 flips the headline, the headline is downgraded to schedule-specific.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Models, data, seeds, budget (and the shrink path)\n\n**Main grid:** Model **A** (GQA 4:1, SwiGLU 4×, tied, `B=17` blocks) and Model **B** (GQA 6:1, SwiGLU 5.83×, deeper `L=28`, `B=29`) — chosen to span GQA ratio, MLP ratio, depth and vocab, which is what \"across various transformer modules\" needs for the claim to be non-architectural. **≥ 3 seeds** per cell. **Data:** short instruction finetuning (≈ 50 M tokens, `T ≈ 1560` steps at 64 × 512-token batches) as the primary condition, plus **one continued-pretraining condition** (≈ 200 M tokens, Model A, 3 seeds) to test whether conclusions are condition-specific.\n\n**Model C is a conditional structural-coverage arm**, not a headline: it is the only arm that exercises MHA, fused-QKV splitting and an **untied LM head**, and all 122 of its tensors sit exactly at the `min-dim = 1024` exponent threshold.\n\n**Budget (idealized `6·N·tokens` at an assumed 180 TFLOP/s effective — *hypothetical run environment*, this host has no GPU):** instr-FT run ≈ 0.59–0.72 GPU-h → 30 main-grid runs ≈ **20 GPU-h**; CP arm 15 runs ≈ **34 GPU-h**; 7B descriptive arm (5 arms × 1 seed) ≈ **15 GPU-h**. Wall-clock for BAdam is expected to be *worse* than this FLOP estimate (full backward per step, per-block state shuffling); budget 2–4×. Storage from the retention scheme in §3: **≈ 16.3 GB/run (A)**, **≈ 20.2 GB/run (B)** → main grid ≈ **0.55 TB**, CP ≈ 0.37 TB, 7B ≈ 0.39 TB, total ≈ **1.3 TB** (+ one shared `W₀` per model).\n\n**7B arm is explicitly conditional and must not be silently assumed.** Its purpose is a single descriptive point at scale (does the verdict direction survive doubling depth/width?). **Drop order if unaffordable: (1) block-granularity ablations, (2) block-ordering ablation, (3) the continued-pretraining condition's seed count 3→2, (4) Model C, (5) then — only as a last resort — the 7B arm is dropped, and the paper states the scale claim is not tested.** If ablations 1–2 are dropped instead of the 7B arm, the mechanism-attribution section must be downgraded accordingly, because the 7B point cannot substitute for the ablations.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Analysis-code contract (deliverable in a real environment; none of it runnable here)\n\n```\nmanifest.py     write_manifest(run_id, arm, seed, model_cfg, lr, block_cfg, data_hash, W0_hash)\n                # verify W0_hash identical across all arms of a model; abort otherwise\nslicing.py      slice_modules(state_dict, arch_cfg) -> list[ModuleSpec]\n                ModuleSpec(path, kind, layer, shape, min_dim, qualifies: EXPONENT|RANK|SUPPRESSED,\n                           per_head: Optional[(n_heads, head_dim)], split_fused: bool)\ncapture.py      DeltaRecorder(W0, grid, full_at, p3_windows)   # P1/P2/P3 per §3, fp32\nspectra.py      singular_values(M, mode='exact'|'lowrank_topk', k) -> (s, U_k, V_k)\ndescriptors.py  describe(s, W0_s=None) -> dict(r_eff, PR, stable_rank, spec_entropy, norm_entropy,\n                           max_over_mean, cond, tail_mass_topk, alpha_mle, alpha_logbin,\n                           bulk_slope, head_slope, x_min, ks, aic_table, flags)\n                # FLAGS first: RANK_DEFICIENT / NOT_ASSESSABLE / INSUFFICIENT_SUPPORT\npowerlaw.py     cs_mle(s2) , choose_xmin_ks(s2) , logbinned_ccdf(s2, nbins) ,\n                nulls(s2) -> {'pl','lognormal','trunc_pl','exp'} -> AIC/BIC\nnulls_spectral.py mp_surrogate(m, n, fro, seed); displaced_W0_null(W0, fro, seed);\n                  equal_update_surrogate(n_updates, m, n, per_step_fro, seed)\nalignment.py    subspace_overlap(A, B, k) -> (overlap, random_null_p); offdiag_mass(U0ᵀ ΔW V0)\nstats.py        cluster_bootstrap(df, cluster='layer', strata='kind', B=10000)\n                tost(ci, lo, hi) -> {'EQUIV','NON-EQUIV','INCONCLUSIVE'}\n                mixed_model(df) -> fixed effects incl. kind×arm interaction\nverdict.py      heavy_tail(cell) -> {YES,NO,UNSTABLE,DRIFTING,RANK_DEFICIENT,NOT_ASSESSABLE}\n                comparability(cell) -> {COMPARABLE,NOT,INCONCLUSIVE} + heterogeneity override\n                headline(p1, p3, alpha, rank) -> per §9 matrix  # single source of truth\nreport.py       tables: per-module distributions, in/out-of-band counts by kind, covariate columns\n```\n**Required unit tests before any real run** (these validate the plumbing only, and will be reported as such, never as findings): analytic spectra — diagonal matrix with prescribed `σ`; exactly low-rank matrix (`PR` exact, `r_eff` exact); Pareto-generated `s²` with known α (recovery within 5% at `p = 4096`); log-normal-generated `s²` (must be *rejected* by R1); Wishart (must match the Marchenko–Pastur surrogate); scale invariance (`describe(c·M) == describe(M)`); rotation invariance; degenerate inputs (zero matrix, `rank 1`, `p = 1`).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Feasibility (tied to observation, not aspiration)\n\n- **This host cannot execute any part of the above.** No scientific Python stack (numpy/scipy/torch/transformers absent), no network and no pip cache (no install path), no GPU. The design is therefore delivered as a specification; the §11/§12 numbers are configuration arithmetic, not measurements.\n- The **224 CPU / ~2 TB RAM** observation is labelled *hypothetical run-environment context only*. It is relevant for one thing: the SVD analysis stage would be CPU-tractable — a full-SVD sweep of all 113 modules of Model A at one checkpoint ≈ **4.4 × 10¹³ FLOP ≈ 2200 core-seconds single-threaded, ~110 s with 64-way BLAS** (Model B: 3.8 × 10¹³ FLOP, ~95 s), i.e. the analysis cost is negligible against training even without a GPU. Training is not.\n- **Interpreter risk:** python 3.14 wheel availability for the torch/transformers/peft stack is unknown from here. A real run environment should pin python 3.11 and record `pip freeze` in the manifest.\n- **BAdam's memory advantage is not free here:** arm 4 and arm 1 differ in state allocation, so a per-run memory cap must be recorded and reported; if the 7B arm forces different precision for Adam states, that arm becomes descriptive-only (stated in advance, not discovered later).\n- **If artifacts ever appear** (a BAdam implementation, or existing runs with optimizer state), the pinned §2 spec is replaced by the actual implementation, assumptions A1–A9 become observed facts with recorded values, and if checkpoints with weights at multiple steps exist, arms 1–2 could be replaced by descriptor estimation alone — the current inventory contains none.\n\n## Assumptions vs. fixed requirements\n\n**Fixed by the task:** the question as stated; design-only scope (`task.json`: \"repositories and compute are not required\"); environment as observed.\n**Design assumptions (mine, all falsifiable by ablation):** everything in §2's table A1–A9; the model choices A/B (and conditional C, conditional 7B); dataset scale and step count; the `min-dim ≥ 1024` exponent threshold and `min-dim ≥ 32` analysis floor; per-head treatment of GQA `k`/`v`; fp32 perturbation retention with 12-point grid / 3 full-matrix dumps / 48 P3 windows; the R1–R6 acceptance rules with ΔAIC ≥ 10 and ≥ 10% tail coverage; rank-weighting by `min(m,n)`; the 180 TFLOP/s throughput assumption used only in §12.\n**Open, to be resolved by data, not by me:** every descriptor value, every verdict, and whether the two conjuncts of the question hold at all. No expected result is presented anywhere in this document as a finding, and no experiment has been executed."}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-0875aee7-ccce-94f3-9590-3d522038232a", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": null, "kind": "ActionEvent"}
