{"id": "e99e02cb-8c02-491a-aa9a-ad3433b85b72", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T11:24:45.918865", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "6b3e3662-94e2-45a3-868a-c1a65d4cff21", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "I need to include evidence, assumptions, uncertainties. Let me resubmit with evidence field.
", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-996b66ed9b09d925", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-996b66ed9b09d925", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"stage": "result", "answer": "# EXPERIMENT DESIGN (specification only — nothing has been run)\n\n**Question.** Does BAdam produce a learning perturbation with a heavy-tailed singular-value distribution and an effective rank comparable to Adam's, across transformer modules during finetuning?\n\n**The question is a conjunction.** A \"yes\" requires, per module family: (i) the perturbation's spectrum is heavy-tailed by a pre-registered fitting standard, **and** (ii) its effective rank is statistically *equivalent* (not merely \"not significantly different\") to Adam's. The headline verdict is a three-valued AND (YES / NO / INCONCLUSIVE) over those conjuncts, with a mandatory per-module-type breakdown. If only one conjunct holds, that is reported as the answer, not smoothed into a single number.\n\n**Nothing in this document is an experimental result.** All arithmetic below is specification arithmetic (module counts, storage, FLOPs) computed on this host from configuration numbers; no training, no SVD of any real weight matrix, no outcome.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Environment reality (observed) — carries into §12\n- `/workspace` contains **only `task.json`**. No BAdam/block-coordinate code, no Adam/AdamW implementation, no finetuning scripts, no model configs, no SVD/spectral utilities, no checkpoints or update artifacts (filesystem-wide `find`: 0 hits for `*badam*`, `*finetune*`, `*.safetensors|*.pt|*.ckpt`). `task.json` itself states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\", `datasets: []`.\n- Interpreter `python3 3.14.4`; **no** numpy/scipy/torch/transformers/peft/datasets/accelerate/matplotlib/pandas/sklearn/powerlaw; **no network** (DNS resolution fails), empty pip cache → **no install path**. **No GPU** (`nvidia-smi` fails).\n- Consequence: the optimizer in §2 is **specified by me in pinned pseudo-code**, not imported from a repo, and every blocking parameter is an explicit design assumption.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Rationale: what the design must actually discriminate\n- **(C1) Heavy tail** of the perturbation spectrum — a shape property of one matrix's singular values, confounded by the pretrained spectrum (§5).\n- **(C2) Effective-rank comparability** — a paired, cross-optimizer equivalence statement on the same starting weights.\n- **(C3) Mechanism attribution** — BAdam's distinguishing feature is **temporal sparsity of updates**: one block updated per step, so each tensor receives ≈ `T/B` applied updates instead of `T`, with Adam moments that go stale between activations. The block structure is over **parameter groups**, not a factorization inside a matrix. Hence the load-bearing control is a **step-thinned AdamW** arm (full-model update every `B` steps): without it, any effective-rank deficit is unattributable.\n\nThe mechanism (C3) lives in the *step* matrices (P3); the question is about the *accumulated* perturbation (P1). §9 fixes in advance what happens if they disagree.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Pinned optimizer specification (assumption-pinned, not repo-pinned)\n```\n# BAdam-full — pinned specification. Every constant is an assumption (A1..A9),\n# NOT a property of any canonical implementation (no code exists in /workspace).\ninit: W_0 = pretrained weights; AdamW state (m,v) allocated ONLY for the active block\nblocks: ordered list [Bl_1 .. Bl_B]                               # A1 granularity\n    Bl_U = {embed_tokens, lm_head(unembedding), all 1-D params}    # A5 unembedding handling\n    Bl_l = {q,k,v,o,gate,up,down of layer l}, l = L-1 .. 0         # A2 order: reverse/top-down\n           (+ layer-norm gains of layer l)                          # A6 norms ride their layer block\nfor t = 1..T:                                                     # T = matched step count\n    if t <= WARMUP: active = ALL blocks                            # A3 warmup\n    else:           active = cycle over blocks, Bl_U visited once per cycle-end\n    g = backward(full batch)          # FULL fwd+bwd every step: gradients for all params\n    for p in active: p, m_p, v_p = adamw_step(p, g[p], m_p, v_p, lr_t,\n                                             b1=0.9, b2=0.999, eps=1e-8, weight_decay=0.0)  # A4\n    # inactive p: untouched (m_p, v_p not allocated / not updated)   # A7 no implicit decay\n    lr_t = cosine decay, linear warmup over 3% of T                  # A8 schedule (matched across arms)\nclip: global grad-norm 1.0 over the FULL gradient (identical across all arms)\n```\n| # | Blocking parameter | Pinned | Ablation alternatives |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A1 | granularity | per layer + one unembedding block | per-tensor; module-group; random partition; whole-model (≡AdamW) |\n| A2 | ordering | reverse topological, cyclic | forward; random |\n| A3 | warmup | 10 full-model steps | 0; `B` steps |\n| A4 | base optimizer in block | AdamW β=(0.9,0.999), ε=1e-8, wd=0.0 | wd=0.1 |\n| A5 | unembedding handling | own block, once per cycle | excluded; merged with layer L-1 |\n| A6 | norm/1-D params | attached to their layer's block | own blocks |\n| A7 | stale-state policy | frozen between activations | refresh on activation |\n| A8 | LR/schedule | cosine, 3% warmup, best of {1e-5,2e-5,5e-5} per arm | fixed shared LR |\n| A9 | steps per block | 1 | 2; 4 |\n\nModel A (`L=16`): `B = 17` blocks; with `T ≈ 1560` steps (§12), **each tensor gets ≈ 92 applied updates** under BAdam vs 1560 under AdamW — the 17× gap control arm 4 exists to isolate.\n**Compute honesty:** BAdam does a full fwd+bwd every step and discards ≈`(B−1)/B` of computed gradients; it saves optimizer memory, not FLOPs. Matched-step is therefore FLOP-honest and a BAdam disadvantage is not a compute-budget artifact.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The three perturbation objects (per 2-D tensor, per run/seed)\n| id | definition | role | retention |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **P1** | `ΔW = W_t − W_0` | **primary / headline** | full matrix at 3 checkpoints; spectra + top-64 singular vectors at all 12 grid points |\n| **P2** | `U_[t1,t2] = W_t2 − W_t1` | interval view; early dynamics not washed out by accumulation | derived from adjacent P1 snapshots |\n| **P3** | `S_w = Σ_{t∈window}(p_before − p_after)` | **explanatory** — direct view of temporal sparsity | 48 windows/run; spectra computed inline, matrices discarded |\n| **W₀** | pretrained weights | reference spectrum + confound control (§5) | stored **once per model**, shared by all arms/seeds |\n\nfp32 for all stored quantities; bf16 compute; one fp32 master copy per parameter in **every** arm, so precision cannot masquerade as a spectral difference.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Module slicing (counts computed from configs)\nOnly **2-D** tensors enter spectral claims.\n\n| Model | h | L | inter | vocab | GQA | 2-D tensors | exponent-qualified (min-dim ≥1024) | rank-only (32≤min-dim<1024) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **A** Llama-3.2-1B-class (SwiGLU 4×, tied) | 2048 | 16 | 8192 | 128 256 | 32q/8kv | 113 | **81** | 32 (all `k`/`v`, 512×2048) |\n| **B** Qwen2.5-1.5B-class (SwiGLU 5.83×, tied) | 1536 | 28 | 8960 | 151 936 | 12q/2kv | 197 | **141** | 56 (all `k`/`v`, 128×1536) |\n| **C** Pythia-410M-class (MHA, fused QKV, untied head) — *conditional* | 1024 | 24 | 4096 | 50 304 | 16/16 | 122 | **122** | 0 |\n\nReporting bins crossed in every table: module type (`q,k,v,o,gate,up,down,embed,lm_head`) × depth bin (early/mid/late + per-layer curves) × model × arm × seed × grid point × object (P1/P2/P3).\nCarried forward unchanged:\n- **GQA `k`/`v`** never pooled as one rectangular matrix → **per-head** `[64 × h]` spectra (A: 8×16×2 = 256 matrices; B: 2×28×2 = 112); a stacked descriptor is labelled as such. `min-dim = 64` ⇒ rank descriptors only, **never exponents**.\n- **Fused QKV (C)** split along rows into q/k/v thirds before analysis, split recorded in the manifest.\n- **Embeddings / LM head** analysed as their own bin, never pooled into the per-layer aggregate.\n- **Norm gains, biases, 1-D**: **descriptive only** — no effective-rank claim; report `‖Δθ‖₂/‖θ₀‖₂`, coordinate dispersion, Gini of |Δθ|. Headline scoped to \"2-D projection modules\".\n- **Sample-rank-deficient flag**: `PR < 8` or `rank < 8` ⇒ `RANK_DEFICIENT`, report rank fraction, suppress exponent and ratio claims.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The W₀ confound (not a footnote)\n1. **`W₀` shared across arms** — the paired P1 comparison (same `W₀`, seed, data order) cancels most of the confound. Report **both** the paired difference (confound-cancelled) **and** absolute values (confound-present); never conclude from the absolute column alone.\n2. **Alignment test** — principal angles between top-`k=64` left/right singular subspaces of `ΔW` and `W₀`, scored against a matched-spectrum random-rotation null, plus off-diagonal energy of `U₀ᵀ ΔW V₀`. High overlap ⇒ tail inherited from `W₀`'s geometry; low overlap ⇒ intrinsic.\n3. **Inheritance regression** — regress `α(ΔW)` on `α(W₀)` across modules, per arm; slope ≈ 1 with matching intercepts in both arms means heavy tails are inherited and are not a discriminator.\n4. **Displaced-copy null** `ΔW_null = R₁ diag(S(W₀)) R₂ᵀ` rescaled to `‖ΔW‖_F`: defines exactly what \"heavy-tailed because `W₀` is\" looks like; `ΔW` must beat it to support C1.\n5. **`‖ΔW‖_F/‖W₀‖_F` is a required column in every comparison table**, with `α(W₀)`, `r_eff(W₀)`, `PR(W₀)`, `‖W₀‖_F`; it also enters the mixed model as a covariate and stratifies the band plots (descriptor comparison means different things at 0.1% vs 5% relative displacement).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Descriptor set (from `σ₁ ≥ … ≥ σ_p > 0`, applied to `s² = σ²`)\n**Rank/concentration:** `r_eff = exp(−Σ pᵢ ln pᵢ)`, `pᵢ = σᵢ/Σσ` (Roy–Vetterli); **PR** `= (Σσ²)²/Σσ⁴`; **stable rank**; normalized spectral entropy; `max/mean`; bulk-to-edge; condition number. `r_eff` and `PR` both reported, both must pass equivalence — they disagree in exactly the regime at issue.\n**Tail shape:** α_MLE (Clauset–Shalizi–Newman on `s²`, KS-chosen `x_min`, 1000-resample bootstrap CI); α_logbin (log-binned CCDF slope, 60 bins, HC errors); **two-regime bulk/head slope + crossover** (a single α oversimplifies); tail mass above `x_min`, above a Marchenko–Pastur-derived edge, and top-`k` energy for `k ∈ {1,4,16,64}`.\n**Competing-null fit table:** power law vs log-normal, truncated power law, exponential, AIC/BIC with the R1 margin.\n**Covariates in every table:** `‖ΔW‖_F/‖W₀‖_F`; applied-update count for that tensor (`T` vs `≈T/B`); distinct updates accumulated; dims; `W₀` descriptors; loss and step index; BAdam cycle phase at the snapshot.\n**Spectral nulls (computed, not assumed):** matched-norm Gaussian/MP surrogate; displaced-`W₀` null; **equal-update-count surrogate** (sum of `n_updates` i.i.d. matrices with matched per-step norm) — this separates genuine low-rank structure from small-sample rank deficiency, the central technical trap when comparing a 92-update tensor to a 1560-update one.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Checkpoint grid and heavy-tail acceptance rules\n**Grid (12 points/run):** dense early `t ∈ {10,25,50}`, then fractions of `T` at **1,3,5,10,25,50,75,100 %**, plus the best-dev-loss checkpoint (may coincide). Full matrices at **{25,75,100} %**; spectra + top-64 vectors at all 12; `W₀` and data-order hashed once per run. **48** P3 windows (log-spaced, denser early).\n\n**Heavy-tailed = TRUE only if ALL of R1–R6 pass; failures are counted, never averaged:**\n- **R1 fit quality** — power law must beat log-normal / truncated PL / exponential by **ΔAIC ≥ 10** → else `NOT_HEAVY_TAILED(best-null-wins)`.\n- **R2 tail coverage** — region above `x_min` must hold **≥10 % of the `s²` points and ≥10 % of the `s²` mass**, ≥50 points. Concretely `min-dim=2048` needs ≥205 tail points, `1536` needs ≥154 → else `INSUFFICIENT_SUPPORT`.\n- **R3 drift** — Spearman |ρ(α,t)| ≥ 0.8 or total drift > 0.5 ⇒ `DRIFTING`, which **rejects** the claim (an unstable shape is not a property of the perturbation).\n- **R4 checkpoint instability (researcher-required)** — verdict differing between **any two** grid points ⇒ `UNSTABLE`, **its own counted column**, excluded from the \"heavy-tailed\" numerator, never averaged into a fractional score.\n- **R5 rank deficiency** — `PR < 8` or `rank < 8` ⇒ `RANK_DEFICIENT`.\n- **R6 degeneracy** — `x_min` above the 90th percentile, or `α ∉ [1.01,12]` ⇒ `NOT_ASSESSABLE`.\n\n**Comparability (approved, pre-registered, TOST not failure-to-reject):** `log r_eff` and `log PR` ratio CIs **entirely inside [0.8, 1.25]**; `Δα = α_BAdam − α_AdamW` CI **inside [−0.5,+0.5]**; aggregated as a **rank-weighted** mean log-ratio over qualifying 2-D modules (weight `min(m,n)`; sensitivity under equal and depth-balanced weights — a verdict that flips under reweighting is reported as verdict-fragile); CI-based TOST plus the two one-sided p-values.\n**Mandatory disaggregation:** per-module ratio distributions (ECDF + per-layer curves), **counts of modules in / above / below band by module type**, and a formal **module-type × optimizer interaction test**. An aggregate that passes while attention is above band and MLP below is labelled **PARTIAL/HETEROGENEOUS** and **overrides** the aggregate in the headline.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Arms, alignment, statistics\n| # | arm | purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **BAdam-full** (§2) | object of the question |\n| 2 | **AdamW**, own best LR | primary reference (\"Adam\") |\n| 3 | **AdamW at BAdam's LR** | separates mechanism from step size |\n| 4 | **Step-thinned AdamW** — full-model update only every `B` steps, no block structure | **required mechanism control** (matched applied-update count ≈T/B); without it \"BAdam differs\" ≠ \"block scheduling matters\" |\n| 5 | **SGD-momentum**, best LR | negative control: the pipeline must detect a known non-comparable optimizer |\n\nLR selection from a 3-config, 1-seed preliminary sweep chosen on dev loss **before** any spectral analysis; chosen LR recorded.\n**Alignment:** matched step = **primary** (same index, same example order); matched loss = **secondary**, interpolated, **dropped with an explicit note if trajectories never overlap** (the drop is stated in the results); headline claims made only at matched step.\n**Statistics:** unit = module `(type, layer)`, paired across arms by module identity and seed. Mixed model `log-ratio ~ kind × depth_bin + norm_ratio + applied_updates`, seed random intercepts, **cluster bootstrap over layers stratified by kind** (10 000 resamples). **Primary family** = {`r_eff` ratio, `PR` ratio} on P1 at 50 % and 100 % for 1-vs-2, Holm-corrected, α=0.05; everything else labelled secondary. Seeds ≥3 (5 if affordable); observed between-seed spread reported as a limitation — no power numbers invented.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Pre-registered verdict table, and where P1 and P3 come apart\nHeadline table, one row per model, filled from data and never from expectation: `heavy-tailed(BAdam) | heavy-tailed(AdamW) | Δα comparable | r_eff comparable | PR comparable | heterogeneity override | R2/R4/R5 failure counts by kind | headline AND (YES/NO/INCONCLUSIVE)`.\n\n**P1-vs-P3 rule, fixed in advance** (P1 = headline because the question is about the perturbation; P3 = explanation because the mechanism is temporal sparsity). If they disagree, **the answer is not a plain \"comparable\"**:\n\n| P1 (accumulated) | P3 (step windows) | Pre-declared reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| comparable | comparable | **YES**, mechanism consistent: block-wise Adam steps integrate to an Adam-like displacement. |\n| comparable | **not** comparable | **YES on endpoint, NO on mechanism.** Headline must read: *\"the accumulated perturbation is spectrally comparable to Adam's, but the per-window updates are not; comparability is a consequence of accumulation over the cycle, not of equivalent update 