D. Researcher directives implemented verbatim: full-parameter primary with LoRA separated into its own verdict table; TOST bands [0.8,1.25] and ±0.5 with per-module ratio distributions and in/out-of-band counts by module type plus a kind×arm interaction override; matched-step primary, mandatory step-thinned AdamW arm, matched-loss secondary with explicit drop note, MP null and SGD negative control retained; R1-R4 including the added checkpoint-flip ⇒ UNSTABLE counted-not-averaged rule; two ~0.5-1.5B models / ≥3 seeds / instruction + one continued-pretraining condition with a conditional 7B and an explicit drop order (block-granularity ablations before model count); pinned pseudo-code + A1-A9 assumption table + ablations ranked by overturning power; P1 headline vs P3 explanation with the four-cell pre-declared disagreement table; W_0 treated as a first-class confound with the norm-ratio covariate in every table; finite-size restrictions and the storage/sampling scheme carried forward unchanged.", "assumptions": ["No code or checkpoints exist, so the BAdam variant is specified by the design (pinned pseudo-code plus assumption table A1-A9) rather than imported: full-parameter bi-structured block coordinate descent, per-layer blocks plus one unembedding block, reverse/top-down cyclic order, one step per block, 10 full-model warmup steps, AdamW as in-block base optimizer, frozen stale moments.", "LoRA/B-AdamW is a separate exploratory arm whose low effective rank is constructional and therefore excluded from the main verdict table.", "Primary perturbation object is P1 = W_t - W_0 (accumulated displacement); P3 window-sum step matrices are the mechanistic explanation, and the two are reported side by side with a pre-declared disagreement rule.", "Spectral claims restricted to 2-D tensors: exponent claims require min-dim >= 1024, rank descriptors require >= 32, GQA k/v analysed per-head (min-dim 64, never exponent-fitted), fused QKV split into thirds, embeddings/LM head as their own bin, norms/biases/1-D descriptive only.", "Heavy-tail fitting operates on squared singular values with KS-chosen x_min, with log-normal, truncated power law and exponential as competing nulls at a pre-registered ΔAIC >= 10 margin, a >=10% tail-coverage floor, and drift/checkpoint-flip rejection conditions.", "Comparable means CI-based TOST equivalence: r_eff and PR ratio CIs inside [0.8, 1.25], Δα CI inside ±0.5, rank-weighted by min(m,n), with heterogeneity overriding the aggregate.", "Matched-step alignment is primary and matched-loss secondary; per-arm best LRs come from a preliminary dev-loss sweep fixed before spectral analysis.", "Models A/B (conditional C and conditional 7B) plus a ~50M-token instruction condition and one ~200M-token continued-pretraining condition are my choices, since the task pins no model and provides no dataset.", "Retention plan: fp32 P1 full matrices at 3 grid points, full spectra plus top-64 singular vectors at 12 grid points, 48 P3 windows analysed inline; W_0 stored once per model and shared across arms.", "The 180 TFLOP/s throughput and 20x BLAS speedup used in the budget are planning assumptions for a hypothetical run environment, not measurements from this host.", "No experiment was executed; no descriptor value, verdict or expected outcome is asserted anywhere in the design."], "uncertainties": ["Every scientific quantity is unknown by construction: whether the perturbation is heavy-tailed, whether that shape is stable across checkpoints, and whether effective rank is equivalent between BAdam and AdamW. The design only fixes how these will be decided.", "Whether the heavy-tail conjunct and the comparability conjunct will hold simultaneously; the design reports the conjunction as YES/NO/INCONCLUSIVE rather than averaging the two.", "Whether P1 and P3 will agree; the four-cell interpretation table in §9 covers both directions of disagreement but cannot predict which occurs.", "Whether the step-thinned AdamW control will absorb the BAdam-vs-AdamW gap, which would reclassify any difference as an update-frequency effect rather than a block-scheduling effect - the design's main risk of a null mechanism claim.", "Exact BAdam blocking parameters (A1-A9) are assumptions with no repo to pin; the ranked ablation ladder exists precisely because the headline's robustness rests on A1, A8, A9 and arm 4.", "Real compute and wall-clock availability, and whether the eventual execution environment resolves the python 3.14 wheel question; the 7B arm and Model C are conditional and may be dropped under the stated order.", "Statistical power at 3 seeds under cluster bootstrap over layers; underpowered cells will be reported as a limitation rather than compensated by loosening the equivalence bands.", "Whether optimizer-state precision differences between block-scheduled and full-model arms can be fully neutralized at 7B scale; if not, that arm is descriptive-only by prior agreement."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-871f74cda4d43e42", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-871f74cda4d43e42", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"answer": "# EXPERIMENT DESIGN (specification only \u2014 nothing has been run)\n\n**Question.** Does BAdam produce a learning perturbation with a heavy-tailed singular-value distribution and an effective rank comparable to Adam's, across transformer modules during finetuning?\n\n**The question is a conjunction.** A \"yes\" requires, per module family: (i) the perturbation's spectrum is heavy-tailed by a pre-registered fitting standard, **and** (ii) its effective rank is statistically *equivalent* (not merely \"not significantly different\") to Adam's. The headline verdict is a three-valued AND (YES / NO / INCONCLUSIVE) over those conjuncts, with a mandatory per-module-type breakdown. If only one conjunct holds, that is reported as the answer, not smoothed into a single number.\n\n**Nothing in this document is an experimental result.** All arithmetic below is specification arithmetic (module counts, storage, FLOPs) computed on this host from configuration numbers; no training, no SVD of any real weight matrix, no outcome.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Environment reality (observed) \u2014 carries into \u00a712\n- `/workspace` contains **only `task.json`**. No BAdam/block-coordinate code, no Adam/AdamW implementation, no finetuning scripts, no model configs, no SVD/spectral utilities, no checkpoints or update artifacts (filesystem-wide `find`: 0 hits for `*badam*`, `*finetune*`, `*.safetensors|*.pt|*.ckpt`). `task.json` itself states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\", `datasets: []`.\n- Interpreter `python3 3.14.4`; **no** numpy/scipy/torch/transformers/peft/datasets/accelerate/matplotlib/pandas/sklearn/powerlaw; **no network** (DNS resolution fails), empty pip cache \u2192 **no install path**. **No GPU** (`nvidia-smi` fails).\n- Consequence: the optimizer in \u00a72 is **specified by me in pinned pseudo-code**, not imported from a repo, and every blocking parameter is an explicit design assumption.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Rationale: what the design must actually discriminate\n- **(C1) Heavy tail** of the perturbation spectrum \u2014 a shape property of one matrix's singular values, confounded by the pretrained spectrum (\u00a75).\n- **(C2) Effective-rank comparability** \u2014 a paired, cross-optimizer equivalence statement on the same starting weights.\n- **(C3) Mechanism attribution** \u2014 BAdam's distinguishing feature is **temporal sparsity of updates**: one block updated per step, so each tensor receives \u2248 `T/B` applied updates instead of `T`, with Adam moments that go stale between activations. The block structure is over **parameter groups**, not a factorization inside a matrix. Hence the load-bearing control is a **step-thinned AdamW** arm (full-model update every `B` steps): without it, any effective-rank deficit is unattributable.\n\nThe mechanism (C3) lives in the *step* matrices (P3); the question is about the *accumulated* perturbation (P1). \u00a79 fixes in advance what happens if they disagree.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Pinned optimizer specification (assumption-pinned, not repo-pinned)\n```\n# BAdam-full \u2014 pinned specification. Every constant is an assumption (A1..A9),\n# NOT a property of any canonical implementation (no code exists in /workspace).\ninit: W_0 = pretrained weights; AdamW state (m,v) allocated ONLY for the active block\nblocks: ordered list [Bl_1 .. Bl_B]                               # A1 granularity\n    Bl_U = {embed_tokens, lm_head(unembedding), all 1-D params}    # A5 unembedding handling\n    Bl_l = {q,k,v,o,gate,up,down of layer l}, l = L-1 .. 0         # A2 order: reverse/top-down\n           (+ layer-norm gains of layer l)                          # A6 norms ride their layer block\nfor t = 1..T:                                                     # T = matched step count\n    if t <= WARMUP: active = ALL blocks                            # A3 warmup\n    else:           active = cycle over blocks, Bl_U visited once per cycle-end\n    g = backward(full batch)          # FULL fwd+bwd every step: gradients for all params\n    for p in active: p, m_p, v_p = adamw_step(p, g[p], m_p, v_p, lr_t,\n                                             b1=0.9, b2=0.999, eps=1e-8, weight_decay=0.0)  # A4\n    # inactive p: untouched (m_p, v_p not allocated / not updated)   # A7 no implicit decay\n    lr_t = cosine decay, linear warmup over 3% of T                  # A8 schedule (matched across arms)\nclip: global grad-norm 1.0 over the FULL gradient (identical across all arms)\n```\n| # | Blocking parameter | Pinned | Ablation alternatives |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A1 | granularity | per layer + one unembedding block | per-tensor; module-group; random partition; whole-model (\u2261AdamW) |\n| A2 | ordering | reverse topological, cyclic | forward; random |\n| A3 | warmup | 10 full-model steps | 0; `B` steps |\n| A4 | base optimizer in block | AdamW \u03b2=(0.9,0.999), \u03b5=1e-8, wd=0.0 | wd=0.1 |\n| A5 | unembedding handling | own block, once per cycle | excluded; merged with layer L-1 |\n| A6 | norm/1-D params | attached to their layer's block | own blocks |\n| A7 | stale-state policy | frozen between activations | refresh on activation |\n| A8 | LR/schedule | cosine, 3% warmup, best of {1e-5,2e-5,5e-5} per arm | fixed shared LR |\n| A9 | steps per block | 1 | 2; 4 |\n\nModel A (`L=16`): `B = 17` blocks; with `T \u2248 1560` steps (\u00a712), **each tensor gets \u2248 92 applied updates** under BAdam vs 1560 under AdamW \u2014 the 17\u00d7 gap control arm 4 exists to isolate.\n**Compute honesty:** BAdam does a full fwd+bwd every step and discards \u2248`(B\u22121)/B` of computed gradients; it saves optimizer memory, not FLOPs. Matched-step is therefore FLOP-honest and a BAdam disadvantage is not a compute-budget artifact.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The three perturbation objects (per 2-D tensor, per run/seed)\n| id | definition | role | retention |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **P1** | `\u0394W = W_t \u2212 W_0` | **primary / headline** | full matrix at 3 checkpoints; spectra + top-64 singular vectors at all 12 grid points |\n| **P2** | `U_[t1,t2] = W_t2 \u2212 W_t1` | interval view; early dynamics not washed out by accumulation | derived from adjacent P1 snapshots |\n| **P3** | `S_w = \u03a3_{t\u2208window}(p_before \u2212 p_after)` | **explanatory** \u2014 direct view of temporal sparsity | 48 windows/run; spectra computed inline, matrices discarded |\n| **W\u2080** | pretrained weights | reference spectrum + confound control (\u00a75) | stored **once per model**, shared by all arms/seeds |\n\nfp32 for all stored quantities; bf16 compute; one fp32 master copy per parameter in **every** arm, so precision cannot masquerade as a spectral difference.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Module slicing (counts computed from configs)\nOnly **2-D** tensors enter spectral claims.\n\n| Model | h | L | inter | vocab | GQA | 2-D tensors | exponent-qualified (min-dim \u22651024) | rank-only (32\u2264min-dim<1024) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **A** Llama-3.2-1B-class (SwiGLU 4\u00d7, tied) | 2048 | 16 | 8192 | 128 256 | 32q/8kv | 113 | **81** | 32 (all `k`/`v`, 512\u00d72048) |\n| **B** Qwen2.5-1.5B-class (SwiGLU 5.83\u00d7, tied) | 1536 | 28 | 8960 | 151 936 | 12q/2kv | 197 | **141** | 56 (all `k`/`v`, 128\u00d71536) |\n| **C** Pythia-410M-class (MHA, fused QKV, untied head) \u2014 *conditional* | 1024 | 24 | 4096 | 50 304 | 16/16 | 122 | **122** | 0 |\n\nReporting bins crossed in every table: module type (`q,k,v,o,gate,up,down,embed,lm_head`) \u00d7 depth bin (early/mid/late + per-layer curves) \u00d7 model \u00d7 arm \u00d7 seed \u00d7 grid point \u00d7 object (P1/P2/P3).\nCarried forward unchanged:\n- **GQA `k`/`v`** never pooled as one rectangular matrix \u2192 **per-head** `[64 \u00d7 h]` spectra (A: 8\u00d716\u00d72 = 256 matrices; B: 2\u00d728\u00d72 = 112); a stacked descriptor is labelled as such. `min-dim = 64` \u21d2 rank descriptors only, **never exponents**.\n- **Fused QKV (C)** split along rows into q/k/v thirds before analysis, split recorded in the manifest.\n- **Embeddings / LM head** analysed as their own bin, never pooled into the per-layer aggregate.\n- **Norm gains, biases, 1-D**: **descriptive only** \u2014 no effective-rank claim; report `\u2016\u0394\u03b8\u2016\u2082/\u2016\u03b8\u2080\u2016\u2082`, coordinate dispersion, Gini of |\u0394\u03b8|. Headline scoped to \"2-D projection modules\".\n- **Sample-rank-deficient flag**: `PR < 8` or `rank < 8` \u21d2 `RANK_DEFICIENT`, report rank fraction, suppress exponent and ratio claims.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The W\u2080 confound (not a footnote)\n1. **`W\u2080` shared across arms** \u2014 the paired P1 comparison (same `W\u2080`, seed, data order) cancels most of the confound. Report **both** the paired difference (confound-cancelled) **and** absolute values (confound-present); never conclude from the absolute column alone.\n2. **Alignment test** \u2014 principal angles between top-`k=64` left/right singular subspaces of `\u0394W` and `W\u2080`, scored against a matched-spectrum random-rotation null, plus off-diagonal energy of `U\u2080\u1d40 \u0394W V\u2080`. High overlap \u21d2 tail inherited from `W\u2080`'s geometry; low overlap \u21d2 intrinsic.\n3. **Inheritance regression** \u2014 regress `\u03b1(\u0394W)` on `\u03b1(W\u2080)` across modules, per arm; slope \u2248 1 with matching intercepts in both arms means heavy tails are inherited and are not a discriminator.\n4. **Displaced-copy null** `\u0394W_null = R\u2081 diag(S(W\u2080)) R\u2082\u1d40` rescaled to `\u2016\u0394W\u2016_F`: defines exactly what \"heavy-tailed because `W\u2080` is\" looks like; `\u0394W` must beat it to support C1.\n5. **`\u2016\u0394W\u2016_F/\u2016W\u2080\u2016_F` is a required column in every comparison table**, with `\u03b1(W\u2080)`, `r_eff(W\u2080)`, `PR(W\u2080)`, `\u2016W\u2080\u2016_F`; it also enters the mixed model as a covariate and stratifies the band plots (descriptor comparison means different things at 0.1% vs 5% relative displacement).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Descriptor set (from `\u03c3\u2081 \u2265 \u2026 \u2265 \u03c3_p > 0`, applied to `s\u00b2 = \u03c3\u00b2`)\n**Rank/concentration:** `r_eff = exp(\u2212\u03a3 p\u1d62 ln p\u1d62)`, `p\u1d62 = \u03c3\u1d62/\u03a3\u03c3` (Roy\u2013Vetterli); **PR** `= (\u03a3\u03c3\u00b2)\u00b2/\u03a3\u03c3\u2074`; **stable rank**; normalized spectral entropy; `max/mean`; bulk-to-edge; condition number. `r_eff` and `PR` both reported, both must pass equivalence \u2014 they disagree in exactly the regime at issue.\n**Tail shape:** \u03b1_MLE (Clauset\u2013Shalizi\u2013Newman on `s\u00b2`, KS-chosen `x_min`, 1000-resample bootstrap CI); \u03b1_logbin (log-binned CCDF slope, 60 bins, HC errors); **two-regime bulk/head slope + crossover** (a single \u03b1 oversimplifies); tail mass above `x_min`, above a Marchenko\u2013Pastur-derived edge, and top-`k` energy for `k \u2208 {1,4,16,64}`.\n**Competing-null fit table:** power law vs log-normal, truncated power law, exponential, AIC/BIC with the R1 margin.\n**Covariates in every table:** `\u2016\u0394W\u2016_F/\u2016W\u2080\u2016_F`; applied-update count for that tensor (`T` vs `\u2248T/B`); distinct updates accumulated; dims; `W\u2080` descriptors; loss and step index; BAdam cycle phase at the snapshot.\n**Spectral nulls (computed, not assumed):** matched-norm Gaussian/MP surrogate; displaced-`W\u2080` null; **equal-update-count surrogate** (sum of `n_updates` i.i.d. matrices with matched per-step norm) \u2014 this separates genuine low-rank structure from small-sample rank deficiency, the central technical trap when comparing a 92-update tensor to a 1560-update one.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Checkpoint grid and heavy-tail acceptance rules\n**Grid (12 points/run):** dense early `t \u2208 {10,25,50}`, then fractions of `T` at **1,3,5,10,25,50,75,100 %**, plus the best-dev-loss checkpoint (may coincide). Full matrices at **{25,75,100} %**; spectra + top-64 vectors at all 12; `W\u2080` and data-order hashed once per run. **48** P3 windows (log-spaced, denser early).\n\n**Heavy-tailed = TRUE only if ALL of R1\u2013R6 pass; failures are counted, never averaged:**\n- **R1 fit quality** \u2014 power law must beat log-normal / truncated PL / exponential by **\u0394AIC \u2265 10** \u2192 else `NOT_HEAVY_TAILED(best-null-wins)`.\n- **R2 tail coverage** \u2014 region above `x_min` must hold **\u226510 % of the `s\u00b2` points and \u226510 % of the `s\u00b2` mass**, \u226550 points. Concretely `min-dim=2048` needs \u2265205 tail points, `1536` needs \u2265154 \u2192 else `INSUFFICIENT_SUPPORT`.\n- **R3 drift** \u2014 Spearman |\u03c1(\u03b1,t)| \u2265 0.8 or total drift > 0.5 \u21d2 `DRIFTING`, which **rejects** the claim (an unstable shape is not a property of the perturbation).\n- **R4 checkpoint instability (researcher-required)** \u2014 verdict differing between **any two** grid points \u21d2 `UNSTABLE`, **its own counted column**, excluded from the \"heavy-tailed\" numerator, never averaged into a fractional score.\n- **R5 rank deficiency** \u2014 `PR < 8` or `rank < 8` \u21d2 `RANK_DEFICIENT`.\n- **R6 degeneracy** \u2014 `x_min` above the 90th percentile, or `\u03b1 \u2209 [1.01,12]` \u21d2 `NOT_ASSESSABLE`.\n\n**Comparability (approved, pre-registered, TOST not failure-to-reject):** `log r_eff` and `log PR` ratio CIs **entirely inside [0.8, 1.25]**; `\u0394\u03b1 = \u03b1_BAdam \u2212 \u03b1_AdamW` CI **inside [\u22120.5,+0.5]**; aggregated as a **rank-weighted** mean log-ratio over qualifying 2-D modules (weight `min(m,n)`; sensitivity under equal and depth-balanced weights \u2014 a verdict that flips under reweighting is reported as verdict-fragile); CI-based TOST plus the two one-sided p-values.\n**Mandatory disaggregation:** per-module ratio distributions (ECDF + per-layer curves), **counts of modules in / above / below band by module type**, and a formal **module-type \u00d7 optimizer interaction test**. An aggregate that passes while attention is above band and MLP below is labelled **PARTIAL/HETEROGENEOUS** and **overrides** the aggregate in the headline.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Arms, alignment, statistics\n| # | arm | purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **BAdam-full** (\u00a72) | object of the question |\n| 2 | **AdamW**, own best LR | primary reference (\"Adam\") |\n| 3 | **AdamW at BAdam's LR** | separates mechanism from step size |\n| 4 | **Step-thinned AdamW** \u2014 full-model update only every `B` steps, no block structure | **required mechanism control** (matched applied-update count \u2248T/B); without it \"BAdam differs\" \u2260 \"block scheduling matters\" |\n| 5 | **SGD-momentum**, best LR | negative control: the pipeline must detect a known non-comparable optimizer |\n\nLR selection from a 3-config, 1-seed preliminary sweep chosen on dev loss **before** any spectral analysis; chosen LR recorded.\n**Alignment:** matched step = **primary** (same index, same example order); matched loss = **secondary**, interpolated, **dropped with an explicit note if trajectories never overlap** (the drop is stated in the results); headline claims made only at matched step.\n**Statistics:** unit = module `(type, layer)`, paired across arms by module identity and seed. Mixed model `log-ratio ~ kind \u00d7 depth_bin + norm_ratio + applied_updates`, seed random intercepts, **cluster bootstrap over layers stratified by kind** (10 000 resamples). **Primary family** = {`r_eff` ratio, `PR` ratio} on P1 at 50 % and 100 % for 1-vs-2, Holm-corrected, \u03b1=0.05; everything else labelled secondary. Seeds \u22653 (5 if affordable); observed between-seed spread reported as a limitation \u2014 no power numbers invented.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Pre-registered verdict table, and where P1 and P3 come apart\nHeadline table, one row per model, filled from data and never from expectation: `heavy-tailed(BAdam) | heavy-tailed(AdamW) | \u0394\u03b1 comparable | r_eff comparable | PR comparable | heterogeneity override | R2/R4/R5 failure counts by kind | headline AND (YES/NO/INCONCLUSIVE)`.\n\n**P1-vs-P3 rule, fixed in advance** (P1 = headline because the question is about the perturbation; P3 = explanation because the mechanism is temporal sparsity). If they disagree, **the answer is not a plain \"comparable\"**:\n\n| P1 (accumulated) | P3 (step windows) | Pre-declared reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| comparable | comparable | **YES**, mechanism consistent: block-wise Adam steps integrate to an Adam-like displacement. |\n| comparable | **not** comparable | **YES on endpoint, NO on mechanism.** Headline must read: *\"the accumulated perturbation is spectrally comparable to Adam's, but the per-window updates are not; comparability is a consequence of accumulation over the cycle, not of equivalent update geometry.\"* A P1-only \"yes\" is not admissible. |\n| **not** comparable | comparable | **NO.** Divergence arises during *accumulation* (stale moments, interleaving, drift), not in the step \u2014 this refutes the \"Adam in a block = same thing, slower\" reading and is the most mechanistically informative negative. |\n| not comparable | not comparable | **NO**, straightforwardly. |\n\nIf **both** arms fail C1 (R1/R2), the heavy-tail conjunct is unsupported for either optimizer, the question's premise is reported as not supported, and the comparability numbers cannot rescue a \"yes\".\n\n---\n\n## 10. Secondary arm: LoRA / B-AdamW \u2014 separate table, never merged\nExploratory. LoRA `r \u2208 {8,64}` on all linear modules; AdamW vs block-scheduled B-AdamW over per-layer `A`/`B`. **Stated up front: low rank here is constructional** (`\u0394(AB)` has rank \u2264 2r), so a low effective rank is **not evidence about BAdam's mechanism** and cannot enter the main verdict. Rank-aware reformulation: descriptors on `\u0394(AB)`; report `r_eff/(2r)` (capacity actually used), tail mass within the available `2r`, exponents always annotated with `p = 2r` (severe finite-size caveat at r=8), and comparisons against a **capacity-matched** reference (`r` vs `2r` LoRA) rather than full-dimensional nulls.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Ablations ranked by overturning power\n| rank | ablation | what it can overturn |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | **Step-thinned AdamW (arm 4)** | The whole mechanism attribution: if arms 1 and 4 agree on every descriptor, any BAdam-vs-AdamW gap is *applied-update count*, not block scheduling. Highest power; not optional. |\n| **2** | **Block granularity** (per-layer \u2192 per-tensor / module-group / whole-model) | Whether the conclusion is about BAdam's defining choice or about block-coordinate descent at all. |\n| **3** | **Block ordering** (reverse \u2192 forward \u2192 random) | Whether depth-dependent staleness (last-block-first, rarely revisited) drives per-layer effective-rank patterns; can flip depth-bin signs. |\n| **4** | **Random-block-partition control** | Whether the *bi-structured* partition matters versus any partition of the same coarseness. |\n| **5** | **Steps-per-block {1,2,4}**, warmup {0,10,B} | Dose\u2013response in update sparsity; a monotone trend in `r_eff` ratio vs steps-per-block is the cleanest positive mechanism evidence. |\n| 6 | wd 0.0 \u2192 0.1 | Whether tail-shape differences are weight-decay artifacts. |\n\n**Stated explicitly in the write-up:** the main conclusion rests on A1, A9, A8 and arm 4; A3/A5\u2013A7 are secondary. If ablations 1\u20134 flip the headline, it is downgraded to schedule-specific.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Models, data, seeds, budget, shrink path\n**Main grid:** Model **A** (GQA 4:1, SwiGLU 4\u00d7, `B=17`) and **B** (GQA 6:1, SwiGLU 5.83\u00d7, `L=28`, `B=29`) \u2014 chosen to span GQA ratio, MLP ratio, depth and vocab so \"across various transformer modules\" is non-architectural. **\u22653 seeds**/cell. **Data:** short instruction finetuning (\u224850 M tokens, `T \u2248 1560` steps at 64 \u00d7 512-token batches) as primary, plus **one continued-pretraining condition** (\u2248200 M tokens, Model A, 3 seeds).\n**Model C** is a *conditional structural-coverage* arm, not a headline: the only arm exercising MHA, fused-QKV splitting and an **untied LM head**, with all 122 tensors sitting exactly at the `min-dim = 1024` exponent threshold.\n**Budget** (`6\u00b7N\u00b7tokens` at an assumed 180 TFLOP/s effective \u2014 *hypothetical run environment*, this host has no GPU): instr run \u2248 0.59\u20130.72 GPU-h \u2192 30 main-grid runs \u2248 **20 GPU-h**; CP arm 15 runs \u2248 **34 GPU-h**; 7B descriptive (5 arms \u00d7 1 seed) \u2248 **15 GPU-h**. Wall-clock for BAdam should be budgeted at 2\u20134\u00d7 the FLOP estimate (full backward per step, per-block state shuffling). Storage per \u00a73: **\u224816.3 GB/run (A)**, **\u224820.2 GB/run (B)** \u2192 main grid \u2248 **0.55 TB**, CP \u2248 0.37 TB, 7B \u2248 0.39 TB, total \u2248 **1.3 TB** + one shared `W\u2080` per model.\n**7B arm is explicitly conditional and must not be silently assumed**; it contributes a single descriptive point at scale. **Drop order if unaffordable: (1) block-granularity ablations, (2) block-ordering ablation, (3) CP seeds 3\u21922, (4) Model C, (5) last resort the 7B arm** \u2014 and if (1)\u2013(2) are dropped instead of the 7B arm, the mechanism-attribution section is downgraded, because a scale point cannot substitute for ablations.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Analysis-code contract (deliverable in a real environment; none runnable here)\n```\nmanifest.py     write_manifest(run_id, arm, seed, model_cfg, lr, block_cfg, data_hash, W0_hash)\n                # asserts W0_hash identical across all arms of a model; abort otherwise\nslicing.py      slice_modules(state_dict, arch_cfg) -> [ModuleSpec(path, kind, layer, shape,\n                    min_dim, qualifies: EXPONENT|RANK|SUPPRESSED, per_head, split_fused)]\ncapture.py      DeltaRecorder(W0, grid, full_at, p3_windows)      # P1/P2/P3 per \u00a73, fp32\nspectra.py      singular_values(M, mode='exact'|'lowrank_topk', k) -> (s, U_k, V_k)\ndescriptors.py  describe(s, W0_s=None) -> {r_eff, PR, stable_rank, spec_entropy, norm_entropy,\n                    max_over_mean, cond, tail_mass_topk, alpha_mle, alpha_logbin, bulk_slope,\n                    head_slope, x_min, ks, aic_table, flags}      # FLAGS emitted before values\npowerlaw.py     cs_mle(s2); choose_xmin_ks(s2); logbinned_ccdf(s2,nbins);\n                nulls(s2) -> {pl, lognormal, trunc_pl, exp} -> AIC/BIC\nnulls_spectral.py mp_surrogate(m,n,fro,seed); displaced_W0_null(W0,fro,seed);\n                    equal_update_surrogate(n_updates,m,n,per_step_fro,seed)\nalignment.py    subspace_overlap(A,B,k) -> (overlap, random_null_p); offdiag_mass(U0\u1d40 \u0394W V0)\nstats.py        cluster_bootstrap(df,'layer',strata='kind',B=10000); tost(ci,lo,hi);\n                mixed_model(df)  # includes kind\u00d7arm interaction\nverdict.py      heavy_tail(cell) -> YES|NO|UNSTABLE|DRIFTING|RANK_DEFICIENT|NOT_ASSESSABLE\n                comparability(cell) -> COMPARABLE|NOT|INCONCLUSIVE + heterogeneity override\n                headline(p1,p3,alpha,rank) -> \u00a79 matrix   # single source of truth\nreport.py       per-module distributions; in/out-of-band counts by kind; covariate columns\n```\n**Unit tests required before any real run** (plumbing validation only, reported as such, never as findings): diagonal matrix with prescribed \u03c3; exactly low-rank matrix (exact `PR`/`r_eff`); Pareto-generated `s\u00b2` with known \u03b1 (recovery within 5 % at `p=4096`); log-normal-generated `s\u00b2` (must be *rejected* by R1); Wishart vs the MP surrogate; scale invariance `describe(c\u00b7M)==describe(M)`; rotation invariance; degenerate inputs (zero matrix, rank 1, `p=1`).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Feasibility (tied to observation, not aspiration)\n- **This host cannot execute any part of the above.** No scientific Python stack, no network, no pip cache, no GPU. The deliverable is a specification; \u00a711\u2013\u00a712 numbers are configuration arithmetic, not measurements.\n- The **224 CPU / ~2 TB RAM** observation is labelled *hypothetical run-environment context only*. It matters for one thing: the analysis stage is CPU-tractable \u2014 a full-SVD sweep of all 113 Model-A modules at one checkpoint \u2248 **4.4\u00d710\u00b9\u00b3 FLOP \u2248 2200 core-seconds single-threaded, ~110 s with 64-way BLAS** (Model B: 3.8\u00d710\u00b9\u00b3 FLOP, ~95 s), negligible against training even without a GPU. Training is not.\n- **Interpreter risk:** python 3.14 wheel availability for the torch/transformers/peft stack is unknown from here; a real run environment should pin python 3.11 and record `pip freeze` in the manifest.\n- **BAdam's memory advantage is not free:** arms 1 and 4 differ in state allocation, so per-run memory cap is recorded and reported; if the 7B arm forces different Adam-state precision, that arm becomes descriptive-only \u2014 decided in advance, not discovered later.\n- **If artifacts ever appear** (a BAdam implementation, or existing runs with optimizer state), the \u00a72 spec is replaced by the real implementation, A1\u2013A9 become observed values, and arms 1\u20132 could collapse to descriptor estimation alone. The current inventory contains none.\n\n## Assumptions vs fixed requirements\n**Fixed by the task:** the question as stated; design-only scope (`task.json`: \"repositories and compute are not required\"); the environment as observed.\n**Design assumptions (mine, each falsifiable by ablation):** everything in \u00a72 A1\u2013A9; model choices A/B (conditional C, conditional 7B); dataset scale and step count; `min-dim \u2265 1024` exponent threshold and `\u226532` analysis floor; per-head GQA handling; fp32 retention with a 12-point grid / 3 full-matrix dumps / 48 P3 windows; R1\u2013R6 with \u0394AIC \u2265 10 and \u226510 % tail coverage; `min(m,n)` rank-weighting; the 180 TFLOP/s throughput used only in \u00a712.\n**Left to the data:** every descriptor value and every verdict, including whether either conjunct of the question holds. No expected result is presented as a finding, and nothing was executed.", "assumptions": ["No code or checkpoints exist, so the BAdam variant is specified by the design (pinned pseudo-code plus assumption table A1-A9) rather than imported: full-parameter bi-structured block coordinate descent, per-layer blocks plus one unembedding block, reverse/top-down cyclic order, one step per block, 10 full-model warmup steps, AdamW as in-block base optimizer, frozen stale moments.", "LoRA/B-AdamW is a separate exploratory arm whose low effective rank is constructional and therefore excluded from the main verdict table.", "Primary perturbation object is P1 = W_t - W_0 (accumulated displacement); P3 window-sum step matrices are the mechanistic explanation, and the two are reported side by side with a pre-declared disagreement rule.", "Spectral claims restricted to 2-D tensors: exponent claims require min-dim >= 1024, rank descriptors require >= 32, GQA k/v analysed per-head (min-dim 64, never exponent-fitted), fused QKV split into thirds, embeddings/LM head as their own bin, norms/biases/1-D descriptive only.", "Heavy-tail fitting operates on squared singular values with KS-chosen x_min, with log-normal, truncated power law and exponential as competing nulls at a pre-registered \u0394AIC >= 10 margin, a >=10% tail-coverage floor, and drift/checkpoint-flip rejection conditions.", "Comparable means CI-based TOST equivalence: r_eff and PR ratio CIs inside [0.8, 1.25], \u0394\u03b1 CI inside \u00b10.5, rank-weighted by min(m,n), with heterogeneity overriding the aggregate.", "Matched-step alignment is primary and matched-loss secondary; per-arm best LRs come from a preliminary dev-loss sweep fixed before spectral analysis.", "Models A/B (conditional C and conditional 7B) plus a ~50M-token instruction condition and one ~200M-token continued-pretraining condition are my choices, since the task pins no model and provides no dataset.", "Retention plan: fp32 P1 full matrices at 3 grid points, full spectra plus top-64 singular vectors at 12 grid points, 48 P3 windows analysed inline; W_0 stored once per model and shared across arms.", "The 180 TFLOP/s throughput and 20x BLAS speedup used in the budget are planning assumptions for a hypothetical run environment, not measurements from this host.", "No experiment was executed; no descriptor value, verdict or expected outcome is asserted anywhere in the design."], "evidence": "All observations are read-only inspection plus specification arithmetic. No experiment was executed; no spectral quantity was measured on any real weight matrix.\n\nA. Repository / input inventory\n1. `ls -la /workspace` and `find /workspace -maxdepth 2 -type d`: exactly one file, `task.json` (699 B); zero subdirectories. The masked repository contains no code.\n2. `find / -xdev -iname '*badam*'` \u2192 0 hits; `find / -xdev -iname '*finetune*' -o -iname '*fine_tune*'` \u2192 0 hits. No BAdam/block-coordinate-descent optimizer and no finetuning entrypoint exist anywhere on the image \u2014 which is why \u00a72 pins the optimizer in pseudo-code with assumptions A1-A9 instead of importing it.\n3. `find / -xdev \\( -name '*.safetensors' -o -name '*.pt' -o -name '*.ckpt' \\)` \u2192 0 hits. No checkpoints, weights, optimizer states or logs, so arms 1-2 cannot collapse to descriptor estimation and W_0 must be produced by the run itself.\n4. No SVD/spectral/power-law utility exists to reuse (`powerlaw` also ModuleNotFoundError), motivating the from-spec `powerlaw.py`/`descriptors.py` contract in \u00a713.\n\nB. Environment\n5. `python3 --version` \u2192 3.14.4 (GCC 15.2.0); drives the \u00a714 pin-to-3.11 recommendation.\n6. importlib probe: numpy, scipy, torch, transformers, peft, datasets, accelerate, matplotlib, pandas, sklearn, powerlaw, tqdm all ModuleNotFoundError. `pip3 list` shows only OS packages (ansible 13.1.0, Jinja2 3.1.6, cryptography 46.0.5, ...); PyYAML 6.0.3 present; `/usr/local/lib/python3.14/dist-packages` empty.\n7. Network blocked: `urllib.request.urlopen('https://pypi.org/simple/numpy/')` \u2192 `URLError: [Errno -3] Temporary failure in name resolution`; `pip3 cache list` \u2192 \"No locally built wheels cached.\" \u2192 no install path, so no pilot computation is possible here.\n8. `nvidia-smi` \u2192 \"couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver\"; `gpu_enabled: false`. `nproc` = 224; `free -g` \u2192 2011 GB total / ~1932 GB available \u2014 labelled hypothetical-run-environment context, not a capability claim for this sandbox.\n9. `cat /workspace/task.json` \u2192 `documents.track.md = \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"`, `datasets: []` \u2192 dataset choice is a design assumption.\n\nC. Specification arithmetic computed with stdlib python (configurations, not measurements)\n10. Module inventory per config (2-D tensors only): Model A (h=2048, L=16, inter=8192, vocab=128256, 8 kv heads) \u2192 113 tensors, 81 with min-dim \u2265 1024, 32 rank-only (k/v = 512\u00d72048); shapes {q/o 2048\u00d72048, gate/up 8192\u00d72048, down 2048\u00d78192, k/v 512\u00d72048, embed 128256\u00d72048}. Model B (h=1536, L=28, inter=8960, vocab=151936, 2 kv heads) \u2192 197 tensors, 141 exponent-qualified, 56 rank-only (k/v = 128\u00d71536). Model C (h=1024, L=24, inter=4096, vocab=50304, MHA, fused QKV, untied head) \u2192 122 tensors, all exactly at the min-dim 1024 exponent threshold, with a separate lm_head 50304\u00d71024. These fix the \u00a74 slicing table and the per-head counts (A: 256, B: 112).\n11. Analysis cost: full-SVD sweep over all modules at one checkpoint = 4.4e13 FLOP (Model A) \u2248 2202 single-thread core-seconds at 20 GFLOP/s, ~110 s at an assumed 20\u00d7 BLAS speedup; Model B 3.8e13 FLOP \u2248 1902 / ~95 s. Basis for \u00a714's CPU-tractability statement.\n12. Storage: P1 fp32 snapshot = 4.96 GB (A) / 6.12 GB (B); chosen retention (3 full dumps + full spectra + top-64 vectors at 12 grid points) = 16.31 GB/run (A), 20.23 GB/run (B); P3 inline spectra \u2248 0.02-0.03 GB/run; totals 548 GB main grid + 373 GB CP + 390 GB 7B \u2248 1.31 TB. Basis for the \u00a712 retention scheme and drop order.\n13. Training budget at an assumed 180 TFLOP/s effective: instr-FT (51 M tokens) 0.38-0.47 EFLOP = 0.59-0.72 GPU-h/run; 30 main-grid runs \u2248 19.8 GPU-h; CP (200 M tokens) 15 runs \u2248 34.4 GPU-h; 7B 5 runs \u2248 15.3 GPU-h. Hypothetical run-environment planning context only, with a 2-4\u00d7 wall-clock caveat.\n14. Blocking arithmetic: B = L+1 blocks (A: 17, B: 29); at T \u2248 1560 steps (51 M tokens / (64\u00d7512)) each tensor receives \u2248 92 BAdam updates vs 1560 AdamW updates \u2192 the 17\u00d7 applied-update gap that makes arm 4 mandatory.\n15. Tail-coverage floor in concrete units: 10 % of min-dim 2048 \u2192 \u2265 205 tail points above x_min; min-dim 1536 \u2192 \u2265 154 (rule R2).\n\nD. Researcher directives implemented verbatim: full-parameter primary with LoRA separated into its own verdict table; TOST bands [0.8,1.25] and \u00b10.5 with per-module ratio distributions and in/out-of-band counts by module type plus a kind\u00d7arm interaction override; matched-step primary, mandatory step-thinned AdamW arm, matched-loss secondary with explicit drop note, MP null and SGD negative control retained; R1-R4 including the added checkpoint-flip \u21d2 UNSTABLE counted-not-averaged rule; two ~0.5-1.5B models / \u22653 seeds / instruction + one continued-pretraining condition with a conditional 7B and an explicit drop order (block-granularity ablations before model count); pinned pseudo-code + A1-A9 assumption table + ablations ranked by overturning power; P1 headline vs P3 explanation with the four-cell pre-declared disagreement table; W_0 treated as a first-class confound with the norm-ratio covariate in every table; finite-size restrictions and the storage/sampling scheme carried forward unchanged.", "stage": "result", "uncertainties": ["Every scientific quantity is unknown by construction: whether the perturbation is heavy-tailed, whether that shape is stable across checkpoints, and whether effective rank is equivalent between BAdam and AdamW. The design only fixes how these will be decided.", "Whether the heavy-tail conjunct and the comparability conjunct will hold simultaneously; the design reports the conjunction as YES/NO/INCONCLUSIVE rather than averaging the two.", "Whether P1 and P3 will agree; the four-cell interpretation table in \u00a79 covers both directions of disagreement but cannot predict which occurs.", "Whether the step-thinned AdamW control will absorb the BAdam-vs-AdamW gap, which would reclassify any difference as an update-frequency effect rather than a block-scheduling effect - the design's main risk of a null mechanism claim.", "Exact BAdam blocking parameters (A1-A9) are assumptions with no repo to pin; the ranked ablation ladder exists precisely because the headline's robustness rests on A1, A8, A9 and arm 4.", "Real compute and wall-clock availability, and whether the eventual execution environment resolves the python 3.14 wheel question; the 7B arm and Model C are conditional and may be dropped under the stated order.", "Statistical power at 3 seeds under cluster bootstrap over layers; underpowered cells will be reported as a limitation rather than compensated by loosening the equivalence bands.", "Whether optimizer-state precision differences between block-scheduled and full-model arms can be fully neutralized at 7B scale; if not, that arm is descriptive-only by prior agreement."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-1ef7152f-fb64-4245-a598-88f610dca224", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "end_researcher_turn: {"answer": "# EXPERIMENT DESIGN (specification only \u2014 nothing has been run)\n\n**Question.** Does BAdam produce a learning perturbation with a heavy-tailed singular-value distribution and an effective rank comparable to Adam's, across transformer modules during finetuning?\n\n**The question is a conjunction.** A \"yes\" requires, per module family: (i) the perturbation's spectrum is heavy-tailed by a pre-registered fitting standard, **and** (ii) its effective rank is statistically *equivalent* (not merely \"not significantly different\") to Adam's. The headline verdict is a three-valued AND (YES / NO / INCONCLUSIVE) over those conjuncts, with a mandatory per-module-type breakdown. If only one conjunct holds, that is reported as the answer, not smoothed into a single number.\n\n**Nothing in this document is an experimental result.** All arithmetic below is specification arithmetic (module counts, storage, FLOPs) computed on this host from configuration numbers; no training, no SVD of any real weight matrix, no outcome.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Environment reality (observed) \u2014 carries into \u00a712\n- `/workspace` contains **only `task.json`**. No BAdam/block-coordinate code, no Adam/AdamW implementation, no finetuning scripts, no model configs, no SVD/spectral utilities, no checkpoints or update artifacts (filesystem-wide `find`: 0 hits for `*badam*`, `*finetune*`, `*.safetensors|*.pt|*.ckpt`). `task.json` itself states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\", `datasets: []`.\n- Interpreter `python3 3.14.4`; **no** numpy/scipy/torch/transformers/peft/datasets/accelerate/matplotlib/pandas/sklearn/powerlaw; **no network** (DNS resolution fails), empty pip cache \u2192 **no install path**. **No GPU** (`nvidia-smi` fails).\n- Consequence: the optimizer in \u00a72 is **specified by me in pinned pseudo-code**, not imported from a repo, and every blocking parameter is an explicit design assumption.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Rationale: what the design must actually discriminate\n- **(C1) Heavy tail** of the perturbation spectrum \u2014 a shape property of one matrix's singular values, confounded by the pretrained spectrum (\u00a75).\n- **(C2) Effective-rank comparability** \u2014 a paired, cross-optimizer equivalence statement on the same starting weights.\n- **(C3) Mechanism attribution** \u2014 BAdam's distinguishing feature is **temporal sparsity of updates**: one block updated per step, so each tensor receives \u2248 `T/B` applied updates instead of `T`, with Adam moments that go stale between activations. The block structure is over **parameter groups**, not a factorization inside a matrix. Hence the load-bearing control is a **step-thinned AdamW** arm (full-model update every `B` steps): without it, any effective-rank deficit is unattributable.\n\nThe mechanism (C3) lives in the *step* matrices (P3); the question is about the *accumulated* perturbation (P1). \u00a79 fixes in advance what happens if they disagree.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Pinned optimizer specification (assumption-pinned, not repo-pinned)\n```\n# BAdam-full \u2014 pinned specification. Every constant is an assumption (A1..A9),\n# NOT a property of any canonical implementation (no code exists in /workspace).\ninit: W_0 = pretrained weights; AdamW state (m,v) allocated ONLY for the active block\nblocks: ordered list [Bl_1 .. Bl_B]                               # A1 granularity\n    Bl_U = {embed_tokens, lm_head(unembedding), all 1-D params}    # A5 unembedding handling\n    Bl_l = {q,k,v,o,gate,up,down of layer l}, l = L-1 .. 0         # A2 order: reverse/top-down\n           (+ layer-norm gains of layer l)                          # A6 norms ride their layer block\nfor t = 1..T:                                                     # T = matched step count\n    if t <= WARMUP: active = ALL blocks                            # A3 warmup\n    else:           active = cycle over blocks, Bl_U visited once per cycle-end\n    g = backward(full batch)          # FULL fwd+bwd every step: gradients for all params\n    for p in active: p, m_p, v_p = adamw_step(p, g[p], m_p, v_p, lr_t,\n                                             b1=0.9, b2=0.999, eps=1e-8, weight_decay=0.0)  # A4\n    # inactive p: untouched (m_p, v_p not allocated / not updated)   # A7 no implicit decay\n    lr_t = cosine decay, linear warmup over 3% of T                  # A8 schedule (matched across arms)\nclip: global grad-norm 1.0 over the FULL gradient (identical across all arms)\n```\n| # | Blocking parameter | Pinned | Ablation alternatives |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A1 | granularity | per layer + one unembedding block | per-tensor; module-group; random partition; whole-model (\u2261AdamW) |\n| A2 | ordering | reverse topological, cyclic | forward; random |\n| A3 | warmup | 10 full-model steps | 0; `B` steps |\n| A4 | base optimizer in block | AdamW \u03b2=(0.9,0.999), \u03b5=1e-8, wd=0.0 | wd=0.1 |\n| A5 | unembedding handling | own block, once per cycle | excluded; merged with layer L-1 |\n| A6 | norm/1-D params | attached to their layer's block | own blocks |\n| A7 | stale-state policy | frozen between activations | refresh on activation |\n| A8 | LR/schedule | cosine, 3% warmup, best of {1e-5,2e-5,5e-5} per arm | fixed shared LR |\n| A9 | steps per block | 1 | 2; 4 |\n\nModel A (`L=16`): `B = 17` blocks; with `T \u2248 1560` steps (\u00a712), **each tensor gets \u2248 92 applied updates** under BAdam vs 1560 under AdamW \u2014 the 17\u00d7 gap control arm 4 exists to isolate.\n**Compute honesty:** BAdam does a full fwd+bwd every step and discards \u2248`(B\u22121)/B` of computed gradients; it saves optimizer memory, not FLOPs. Matched-step is therefore FLOP-honest and a BAdam disadvantage is not a compute-budget artifact.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The three perturbation objects (per 2-D tensor, per run/seed)\n| id | definition | role | retention |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **P1** | `\u0394W = W_t \u2212 W_0` | **primary / headline** | full matrix at 3 checkpoints; spectra + top-64 singular vectors at all 12 grid points |\n| **P2** | `U_[t1,t2] = W_t2 \u2212 W_t1` | interval view; early dynamics not washed out by accumulation | derived from adjacent P1 snapshots |\n| **P3** | `S_w = \u03a3_{t\u2208window}(p_before \u2212 p_after)` | **explanatory** \u2014 direct view of temporal sparsity | 48 windows/run; spectra computed inline, matrices discarded |\n| **W\u2080** | pretrained weights | reference spectrum + confound control (\u00a75) | stored **once per model**, shared by all arms/seeds |\n\nfp32 for all stored quantities; bf16 compute; one fp32 master copy per parameter in **every** arm, so precision cannot masquerade as a spectral difference.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Module slicing (counts computed from configs)\nOnly **2-D** tensors enter spectral claims.\n\n| Model | h | L | inter | vocab | GQA | 2-D tensors | exponent-qualified (min-dim \u22651024) | rank-only (32\u2264min-dim<1024) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **A** Llama-3.2-1B-class (SwiGLU 4\u00d7, tied) | 2048 | 16 | 8192 | 128 256 | 32q/8kv | 113 | **81** | 32 (all `k`/`v`, 512\u00d72048) |\n| **B** Qwen2.5-1.5B-class (SwiGLU 5.83\u00d7, tied) | 1536 | 28 | 8960 | 151 936 | 12q/2kv | 197 | **141** | 56 (all `k`/`v`, 128\u00d71536) |\n| **C** Pythia-410M-class (MHA, fused QKV, untied head) \u2014 *conditional* | 1024 | 24 | 4096 | 50 304 | 16/16 | 122 | **122** | 0 |\n\nReporting bins crossed in every table: module type (`q,k,v,o,gate,up,down,embed,lm_head`) \u00d7 depth bin (early/mid/late + per-layer curves) \u00d7 model \u00d7 arm \u00d7 seed \u00d7 grid point \u00d7 object (P1/P2/P3).\nCarried forward unchanged:\n- **GQA `k`/`v`** never pooled as one rectangular matrix \u2192 **per-head** `[64 \u00d7 h]` spectra (A: 8\u00d716\u00d72 = 256 matrices; B: 2\u00d728\u00d72 = 112); a stacked descriptor is labelled as such. `min-dim = 64` \u21d2 rank descriptors only, **never exponents**.\n- **Fused QKV (C)** split along rows into q/k/v thirds before analysis, split recorded in the manifest.\n- **Embeddings / LM head** analysed as their own bin, never pooled into the per-layer aggregate.\n- **Norm gains, biases, 1-D**: **descriptive only** \u2014 no effective-rank claim; report `\u2016\u0394\u03b8\u2016\u2082/\u2016\u03b8\u2080\u2016\u2082`, coordinate dispersion, Gini of |\u0394\u03b8|. Headline scoped to \"2-D projection modules\".\n- **Sample-rank-deficient flag**: `PR < 8` or `rank < 8` \u21d2 `RANK_DEFICIENT`, report rank fraction, suppress exponent and ratio claims.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The W\u2080 confound (not a footnote)\n1. **`W\u2080` shared across arms** \u2014 the paired P1 comparison (same `W\u2080`, seed, data order) cancels most of the confound. Report **both** the paired difference (confound-cancelled) **and** absolute values (confound-present); never conclude from the absolute column alone.\n2. **Alignment test** \u2014 principal angles between top-`k=64` left/right singular subspaces of `\u0394W` and `W\u2080`, scored against a matched-spectrum random-rotation null, plus off-diagonal energy of `U\u2080\u1d40 \u0394W V\u2080`. High overlap \u21d2 tail inherited from `W\u2080`'s geometry; low overlap \u21d2 intrinsic.\n3. **Inheritance regression** \u2014 regress `\u03b1(\u0394W)` on `\u03b1(W\u2080)` across modules, per arm; slope \u2248 1 with matching intercepts in both arms means heavy tails are inherited and are not a discriminator.\n4. **Displaced-copy null** `\u0394W_null = R\u2081 diag(S(W\u2080)) R\u2082\u1d40` rescaled to `\u2016\u0394W\u2016_F`: defines exactly what \"heavy-tailed because `W\u2080` is\" looks like; `\u0394W` must beat it to support C1.\n5. **`\u2016\u0394W\u2016_F/\u2016W\u2080\u2016_F` is a required column in every comparison table**, with `\u03b1(W\u2080)`, `r_eff(W\u2080)`, `PR(W\u2080)`, `\u2016W\u2080\u2016_F`; it also enters the mixed model as a covariate and stratifies the band plots (descriptor comparison means different things at 0.1% vs 5% relative displacement).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Descriptor set (from `\u03c3\u2081 \u2265 \u2026 \u2265 \u03c3_p > 0`, applied to `s\u00b2 = \u03c3\u00b2`)\n**Rank/concentration:** `r_eff = exp(\u2212\u03a3 p\u1d62 ln p\u1d62)`, `p\u1d62 = \u03c3\u1d62/\u03a3\u03c3` (Roy\u2013Vetterli); **PR** `= (\u03a3\u03c3\u00b2)\u00b2/\u03a3\u03c3\u2074`; **stable rank**; normalized spectral entropy; `max/mean`; bulk-to-edge; condition number. `r_eff` and `PR` both reported, both must pass equivalence \u2014 they disagree in exactly the regime at issue.\n**Tail shape:** \u03b1_MLE (Clauset\u2013Shalizi\u2013Newman on `s\u00b2`, KS-chosen `x_min`, 1000-resample bootstrap CI); \u03b1_logbin (log-binned CCDF slope, 60 bins, HC errors); **two-regime bulk/head slope + crossover** (a single \u03b1 oversimplifies); tail mass above `x_min`, above a Marchenko\u2013Pastur-derived edge, and top-`k` energy for `k \u2208 {1,4,16,64}`.\n**Competing-null fit table:** power law vs log-normal, truncated power law, exponential, AIC/BIC with the R1 margin.\n**Covariates in every table:** `\u2016\u0394W\u2016_F/\u2016W\u2080\u2016_F`; applied-update count for that tensor (`T` vs `\u2248T/B`); distinct updates accumulated; dims; `W\u2080` descriptors; loss and step index; BAdam cycle phase at the snapshot.\n**Spectral nulls (computed, not assumed):** matched-norm Gaussian/MP surrogate; displaced-`W\u2080` null; **equal-update-count surrogate** (sum of `n_updates` i.i.d. matrices with matched per-step norm) \u2014 this separates genuine low-rank structure from small-sample rank deficiency, the central technical trap when comparing a 92-update tensor to a 1560-update one.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Checkpoint grid and heavy-tail acceptance rules\n**Grid (12 points/run):** dense early `t \u2208 {10,25,50}`, then fractions of `T` at **1,3,5,10,25,50,75,100 %**, plus the best-dev-loss checkpoint (may coincide). Full matrices at **{25,75,100} %**; spectra + top-64 vectors at all 12; `W\u2080` and data-order hashed once per run. **48** P3 windows (log-spaced, denser early).\n\n**Heavy-tailed = TRUE only if ALL of R1\u2013R6 pass; failures are counted, never averaged:**\n- **R1 fit quality** \u2014 power law must beat log-normal / truncated PL / exponential by **\u0394AIC \u2265 10** \u2192 else `NOT_HEAVY_TAILED(best-null-wins)`.\n- **R2 tail coverage** \u2014 region above `x_min` must hold **\u226510 % of the `s\u00b2` points and \u226510 % of the `s\u00b2` mass**, \u226550 points. Concretely `min-dim=2048` needs \u2265205 tail points, `1536` needs \u2265154 \u2192 else `INSUFFICIENT_SUPPORT`.\n- **R3 drift** \u2014 Spearman |\u03c1(\u03b1,t)| \u2265 0.8 or total drift > 0.5 \u21d2 `DRIFTING`, which **rejects** the claim (an unstable shape is not a property of the perturbation).\n- **R4 checkpoint instability (researcher-required)** \u2014 verdict differing between **any two** grid points \u21d2 `UNSTABLE`, **its own counted column**, excluded from the \"heavy-tailed\" numerator, never averaged into a fractional score.\n- **R5 rank deficiency** \u2014 `PR < 8` or `rank < 8` \u21d2 `RANK_DEFICIENT`.\n- **R6 degeneracy** \u2014 `x_min` above the 90th percentile, or `\u03b1 \u2209 [1.01,12]` \u21d2 `NOT_ASSESSABLE`.\n\n**Comparability (approved, pre-registered, TOST not failure-to-reject):** `log r_eff` and `log PR` ratio CIs **entirely inside [0.8, 1.25]**; `\u0394\u03b1 = \u03b1_BAdam \u2212 \u03b1_AdamW` CI **inside [\u22120.5,+0.5]**; aggregated as a **rank-weighted** mean log-ratio over qualifying 2-D modules (weight `min(m,n)`; sensitivity under equal and depth-balanced weights \u2014 a verdict that flips under reweighting is reported as verdict-fragile); CI-based TOST plus the two one-sided p-values.\n**Mandatory disaggregation:** per-module ratio distributions (ECDF + per-layer curves), **counts of modules in / above / below band by module type**, and a formal **module-type \u00d7 optimizer interaction test**. An aggregate that passes while attention is above band and MLP below is labelled **PARTIAL/HETEROGENEOUS** and **overrides** the aggregate in the headline.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Arms, alignment, statistics\n| # | arm | purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **BAdam-full** (\u00a72) | object of the question |\n| 2 | **AdamW**, own best LR | primary reference (\"Adam\") |\n| 3 | **AdamW at BAdam's LR** | separates mechanism from step size |\n| 4 | **Step-thinned AdamW** \u2014 full-model update only every `B` steps, no block structure | **required mechanism control** (matched applied-update count \u2248T/B); without it \"BAdam differs\" \u2260 \"block scheduling matters\" |\n| 5 | **SGD-momentum**, best LR | negative control: the pipeline must detect a known non-comparable optimizer |\n\nLR selection from a 3-config, 1-seed preliminary sweep chosen on dev loss **before** any spectral analysis; chosen LR recorded.\n**Alignment:** matched step = **primary** (same index, same example order); matched loss = **secondary**, interpolated, **dropped with an explicit note if trajectories never overlap** (the drop is stated in the results); headline claims made only at matched step.\n**Statistics:** unit = module `(type, layer)`, paired across arms by module identity and seed. Mixed model `log-ratio ~ kind \u00d7 depth_bin + norm_ratio + applied_updates`, seed random intercepts, **cluster bootstrap over layers stratified by kind** (10 000 resamples). **Primary family** = {`r_eff` ratio, `PR` ratio} on P1 at 50 % and 100 % for 1-vs-2, Holm-corrected, \u03b1=0.05; everything else labelled secondary. Seeds \u22653 (5 if affordable); observed between-seed spread reported as a limitation \u2014 no power numbers invented.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Pre-registered verdict table, and where P1 and P3 come apart\nHeadline table, one row per model, filled from data and never from expectation: `heavy-tailed(BAdam) | heavy-tailed(AdamW) | \u0394\u03b1 comparable | r_eff comparable | PR comparable | heterogeneity override | R2/R4/R5 failure counts by kind | headline AND (YES/NO/INCONCLUSIVE)`.\n\n**P1-vs-P3 rule, fixed in advance** (P1 = headline because the question is about the perturbation; P3 = explanation because the mechanism is temporal sparsity). If they disagree, **the answer is not a plain \"comparable\"**:\n\n| P1 (accumulated) | P3 (step windows) | Pre-declared reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| comparable | comparable | **YES**, mechanism consistent: block-wise Adam steps integrate to an Adam-like displacement. |\n| comparable | **not** comparable | **YES on endpoint, NO on mechanism.** Headline must read: *\"the accumulated perturbation is spectrally comparable to Adam's, but the per-window updates are not; comparability is a consequence of accumulation over the cycle, not of equivalent update geometry.\"* A P1-only \"yes\" is not admissible. |\n| **not** comparable | comparable | **NO.** Divergence arises during *accumulation* (stale moments, interleaving, drift), not in the step \u2014 this refutes the \"Adam in a block = same thing, slower\" reading and is the most mechanistically informative negative. |\n| not comparable | not comparable | **NO**, straightforwardly. |\n\nIf **both** arms fail C1 (R1/R2), the heavy-tail conjunct is unsupported for either optimizer, the question's premise is reported as not supported, and the comparability numbers cannot rescue a \"yes\".\n\n---\n\n## 10. Secondary arm: LoRA / B-AdamW \u2014 separate table, never merged\nExploratory. LoRA `r \u2208 {8,64}` on all linear modules; AdamW vs block-scheduled B-AdamW over per-layer `A`/`B`. **Stated up front: low rank here is constructional** (`\u0394(AB)` has rank \u2264 2r), so a low effective rank is **not evidence about BAdam's mechanism** and cannot enter the main verdict. Rank-aware reformulation: descriptors on `\u0394(AB)`; report `r_eff/(2r)` (capacity actually used), tail mass within the available `2r`, exponents always annotated with `p = 2r` (severe finite-size caveat at r=8), and comparisons against a **capacity-matched** reference (`r` vs `2r` LoRA) rather than full-dimensional nulls.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Ablations ranked by overturning power\n| rank | ablation | what it can overturn |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1** | **Step-thinned AdamW (arm 4)** | The whole mechanism attribution: if arms 1 and 4 agree on every descriptor, any BAdam-vs-AdamW gap is *applied-update count*, not block scheduling. Highest power; not optional. |\n| **2** | **Block granularity** (per-layer \u2192 per-tensor / module-group / whole-model) | Whether the conclusion is about BAdam's defining choice or about block-coordinate descent at all. |\n| **3** | **Block ordering** (reverse \u2192 forward \u2192 random) | Whether depth-dependent staleness (last-block-first, rarely revisited) drives per-layer effective-rank patterns; can flip depth-bin signs. |\n| **4** | **Random-block-partition control** | Whether the *bi-structured* partition matters versus any partition of the same coarseness. |\n| **5** | **Steps-per-block {1,2,4}**, warmup {0,10,B} | Dose\u2013response in update sparsity; a monotone trend in `r_eff` ratio vs steps-per-block is the cleanest positive mechanism evidence. |\n| 6 | wd 0.0 \u2192 0.1 | Whether tail-shape differences are weight-decay artifacts. |\n\n**Stated explicitly in the write-up:** the main conclusion rests on A1, A9, A8 and arm 4; A3/A5\u2013A7 are secondary. If ablations 1\u20134 flip the headline, it is downgraded to schedule-specific.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Models, data, seeds, budget, shrink path\n**Main grid:** Model **A** (GQA 4:1, SwiGLU 4\u00d7, `B=17`) and **B** (GQA 6:1, SwiGLU 5.83\u00d7, `L=28`, `B=29`) \u2014 chosen to span GQA ratio, MLP ratio, depth and vocab so \"across various transformer modules\" is non-architectural. **\u22653 seeds**/cell. **Data:** short instruction finetuning (\u224850 M tokens, `T \u2248 1560` steps at 64 \u00d7 512-token batches) as primary, plus **one continued-pretraining condition** (\u2248200 M tokens, Model A, 3 seeds).\n**Model C** is a *conditional structural-coverage* arm, not a headline: the only arm exercising MHA, fused-QKV splitting and an **untied LM head**, with all 122 tensors sitting exactly at the `min-dim = 1024` exponent threshold.\n**Budget** (`6\u00b7N\u00b7tokens` at an assumed 180 TFLOP/s effective \u2014 *hypothetical run environment*, this host has no GPU): instr run \u2248 0.59\u20130.72 GPU-h \u2192 30 main-grid runs \u2248 **20 GPU-h**; CP arm 15 runs \u2248 **34 GPU-h**; 7B descriptive (5 arms \u00d7 1 seed) \u2248 **15 GPU-h**. Wall-clock for BAdam should be budgeted at 2\u20134\u00d7 the FLOP estimate (full backward per step, per-block state shuffling). Storage per \u00a73: **\u224816.3 GB/run (A)**, **\u224820.2 GB/run (B)** \u2192 main grid \u2248 **0.55 TB**, CP \u2248 0.37 TB, 7B \u2248 0.39 TB, total \u2248 **1.3 TB** + one shared `W\u2080` per model.\n**7B arm is explicitly conditional and must not be silently assumed**; it contributes a single descriptive point at scale. **Drop order if unaffordable: (1) block-granularity ablations, (2) block-ordering ablation, (3) CP seeds 3\u21922, (4) Model C, (5) last resort the 7B arm** \u2014 and if (1)\u2013(2) are dropped instead of the 7B arm, the mechanism-attribution section is downgraded, because a scale point cannot substitute for ablations.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Analysis-code contract (deliverable in a real environment; none runnable here)\n```\nmanifest.py     write_manifest(run_id, arm, seed, model_cfg, lr, block_cfg, data_hash, W0_hash)\n                # asserts W0_hash identical across all arms of a model; abort otherwise\nslicing.py      slice_modules(state_dict, arch_cfg) -> [ModuleSpec(path, kind, layer, shape,\n                    min_dim, qualifies: EXPONENT|RANK|SUPPRESSED, per_head, split_fused)]\ncapture.py      DeltaRecorder(W0, grid, full_at, p3_windows)      # P1/P2/P3 per \u00a73, fp32\nspectra.py      singular_values(M, mode='exact'|'lowrank_topk', k) -> (s, U_k, V_k)\ndescriptors.py  describe(s, W0_s=None) -> {r_eff, PR, stable_rank, spec_entropy, norm_entropy,\n                    max_over_mean, cond, tail_mass_topk, alpha_mle, alpha_logbin, bulk_slope,\n                    head_slope, x_min, ks, aic_table, flags}      # FLAGS emitted before values\npowerlaw.py     cs_mle(s2); choose_xmin_ks(s2); logbinned_ccdf(s2,nbins);\n                nulls(s2) -> {pl, lognormal, trunc_pl, exp} -> AIC/BIC\nnulls_spectral.py mp_surrogate(m,n,fro,seed); displaced_W0_null(W0,fro,seed);\n                    equal_update_surrogate(n_updates,m,n,per_step_fro,seed)\nalignment.py    subspace_overlap(A,B,k) -> (overlap, random_null_p); offdiag_mass(U0\u1d40 \u0394W V0)\nstats.py        cluster_bootstrap(df,'layer',strata='kind',B=10000); tost(ci,lo,hi);\n                mixed_model(df)  # includes kind\u00d7arm interaction\nverdict.py      heavy_tail(cell) -> YES|NO|UNSTABLE|DRIFTING|RANK_DEFICIENT|NOT_ASSESSABLE\n                comparability(cell) -> COMPARABLE|NOT|INCONCLUSIVE + heterogeneity override\n                headline(p1,p3,alpha,rank) -> \u00a79 matrix   # single source of truth\nreport.py       per-module distributions; in/out-of-band counts by kind; covariate columns\n```\n**Unit tests required before any real run** (plumbing validation only, reported as such, never as findings): diagonal matrix with prescribed \u03c3; exactly low-rank matrix (exact `PR`/`r_eff`); Pareto-generated `s\u00b2` with known \u03b1 (recovery within 5 % at `p=4096`); log-normal-generated `s\u00b2` (must be *rejected* by R1); Wishart vs the MP surrogate; scale invariance `describe(c\u00b7M)==describe(M)`; rotation invariance; degenerate inputs (zero matrix, rank 1, `p=1`).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Feasibility (tied to observation, not aspiration)\n- **This host cannot execute any part of the above.** No scientific Python stack, no network, no pip cache, no GPU. The deliverable is a specification; \u00a711\u2013\u00a712 numbers are configuration arithmetic, not measurements.\n- The **224 CPU / ~2 TB RAM** observation is labelled *hypothetical run-environment context only*. It matters for one thing: the analysis stage is CPU-tractable \u2014 a full-SVD sweep of all 113 Model-A modules at one checkpoint \u2248 **4.4\u00d710\u00b9\u00b3 FLOP \u2248 2200 core-seconds single-threaded, ~110 s with 64-way BLAS** (Model B: 3.8\u00d710\u00b9\u00b3 FLOP, ~95 s), negligible against training even without a GPU. Training is not.\n- **Interpreter risk:** python 3.14 wheel availability for the torch/transformers/peft stack is unknown from here; a real run environment should pin python 3.11 and record `pip freeze` in the manifest.\n- **BAdam's memory advantage is not free:** arms 1 and 4 differ in state allocation, so per-run memory cap is recorded and reported; if the 7B arm forces different Adam-state precision, that arm becomes descriptive-only \u2014 decided in advance, not discovered later.\n- **If artifacts ever appear** (a BAdam implementation, or existing runs with optimizer state), the \u00a72 spec is replaced by the real implementation, A1\u2013A9 become observed values, and arms 1\u20132 could collapse to descriptor estimation alone. The current inventory contains none.\n\n## Assumptions vs fixed requirements\n**Fixed by the task:** the question as stated; design-only scope (`task.json`: \"repositories and compute are not required\"); the environment as observed.\n**Design assumptions (mine, each falsifiable by ablation):** everything in \u00a72 A1\u2013A9; model choices A/B (conditional C, conditional 7B); dataset scale and step count; `min-dim \u2265 1024` exponent threshold and `\u226532` analysis floor; per-head GQA handling; fp32 retention with a 12-point grid / 3 full-matrix dumps / 48 P3 windows; R1\u2013R6 with \u0394AIC \u2265 10 and \u226510 % tail coverage; `min(m,n)` rank-weighting; the 180 TFLOP/s throughput used only in \u00a712.\n**Left to the data:** every descriptor value and every verdict, including whether either conjunct of the question holds. No expected result is presented as a finding, and nothing was executed.", "assumptions": ["No code or checkpoints exist, so the BAdam variant is specified by the design (pinned pseudo-code plus assumption table A1-A9) rather than imported: full-parameter bi-structured block coordinate descent, per-layer blocks plus one unembedding block, reverse/top-down cyclic order, one step per block, 10 full-model warmup steps, AdamW as in-block base optimizer, frozen stale moments.", "LoRA/B-AdamW is a separate exploratory arm whose low effective rank is constructional and therefore excluded from the main verdict table.", "Primary perturbation object is P1 = W_t - W_0 (accumulated displacement); P3 window-sum step matrices are the mechanistic explanation, and the two are reported side by side with a pre-declared disagreement rule.", "Spectral claims restricted to 2-D tensors: exponent claims require min-dim >= 1024, rank descriptors require >= 32, GQA k/v analysed per-head (min-dim 64, never exponent-fitted), fused QKV split into thirds, embeddings/LM head as their own bin, norms/biases/1-D descriptive only.", "Heavy-tail fitting operates on squared singular values with KS-chosen x_min, with log-normal, truncated power law and exponential as competing nulls at a pre-registered \u0394AIC >= 10 margin, a >=10% tail-coverage floor, and drift/checkpoint-flip rejection conditions.", "Comparable means CI-based TOST equivalence: r_eff and PR ratio CIs inside [0.8, 1.25], \u0394\u03b1 CI inside \u00b10.5, rank-weighted by min(m,n), with heterogeneity overriding the aggregate.", "Matched-step alignment is primary and matched-loss secondary; per-arm best LRs come from a preliminary dev-loss sweep fixed before spectral analysis.", "Models A/B (conditional C and conditional 7B) plus a ~50M-token instruction condition and one ~200M-token continued-pretraining condition are my choices, since the task pins no model and provides no dataset.", "Retention plan: fp32 P1 full matrices at 3 grid points, full spectra plus top-64 singular vectors at 12 grid points, 48 P3 windows analysed inline; W_0 stored once per model and shared across arms.", "The 180 TFLOP/s throughput and 20x BLAS speedup used in the budget are planning assumptions for a hypothetical run environment, not measurements from this host.", "No experiment was executed; no descriptor value, verdict or expected outcome is asserted anywhere in the design."], "evidence": "All observations are read-only inspection plus specification arithmetic. No experiment was executed; no spectral quantity was measured on any real weight matrix.\n\nA. Repository / input inventory\n1. `ls -la /workspace` and `find /workspace -maxdepth 2 -type d`: exactly one file, `task.json` (699 B); zero subdirectories. The masked repository contains no code.\n2. `find / -xdev -iname '*badam*'` \u2192 0 hits; `find / -xdev -iname '*finetune*' -o -iname '*fine_tune*'` \u2192 0 hits. No BAdam/block-coordinate-descent optimizer and no finetuning entrypoint exist anywhere on the image \u2014 which is why \u00a72 pins the optimizer in pseudo-code with assumptions A1-A9 instead of importing it.\n3. `find / -xdev \\( -name '*.safetensors' -o -name '*.pt' -o -name '*.ckpt' \\)` \u2192 0 hits. No checkpoints, weights, optimizer states or logs, so arms 1-2 cannot collapse to descriptor estimation and W_0 must be produced by the run itself.\n4. No SVD/spectral/power-law utility exists to reuse (`powerlaw` also ModuleNotFoundError), motivating the from-spec `powerlaw.py`/`descriptors.py` contract in \u00a713.\n\nB. Environment\n5. `python3 --version` \u2192 3.14.4 (GCC 15.2.0); drives the \u00a714 pin-to-3.11 recommendation.\n6. importlib probe: numpy, scipy, torch, transformers, peft, datasets, accelerate, matplotlib, pandas, sklearn, powerlaw, tqdm all ModuleNotFoundError. `pip3 list` shows only OS packages (ansible 13.1.0, Jinja2 3.1.6, cryptography 46.0.5, ...); PyYAML 6.0.3 present; `/usr/local/lib/python3.14/dist-packages` empty.\n7. Network blocked: `urllib.request.urlopen('https://pypi.org/simple/numpy/')` \u2192 `URLError: [Errno -3] Temporary failure in name resolution`; `pip3 cache list` \u2192 \"No locally built wheels cached.\" \u2192 no install path, so no pilot computation is possible here.\n8. `nvidia-smi` \u2192 \"couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver\"; `gpu_enabled: false`. `nproc` = 224; `free -g` \u2192 2011 GB total / ~1932 GB available \u2014 labelled hypothetical-run-environment context, not a capability claim for this sandbox.\n9. `cat /workspace/task.json` \u2192 `documents.track.md = \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"`, `datasets: []` \u2192 dataset choice is a design assumption.\n\nC. Specification arithmetic computed with stdlib python (configurations, not measurements)\n10. Module inventory per config (2-D tensors only): Model A (h=2048, L=16, inter=8192, vocab=128256, 8 kv heads) \u2192 113 tensors, 81 with min-dim \u2265 1024, 32 rank-only (k/v = 512\u00d72048); shapes {q/o 2048\u00d72048, gate/up 8192\u00d72048, down 2048\u00d78192, k/v 512\u00d72048, embed 128256\u00d72048}. Model B (h=1536, L=28, inter=8960, vocab=151936, 2 kv heads) \u2192 197 tensors, 141 exponent-qualified, 56 rank-only (k/v = 128\u00d71536). Model C (h=1024, L=24, inter=4096, vocab=50304, MHA, fused QKV, untied head) \u2192 122 tensors, all exactly at the min-dim 1024 exponent threshold, with a separate lm_head 50304\u00d71024. These fix the \u00a74 slicing table and the per-head counts (A: 256, B: 112).\n11. Analysis cost: full-SVD sweep over all modules at one checkpoint = 4.4e13 FLOP (Model A) \u2248 2202 single-thread core-seconds at 20 GFLOP/s, ~110 s at an assumed 20\u00d7 BLAS speedup; Model B 3.8e13 FLOP \u2248 1902 / ~95 s. Basis for \u00a714's CPU-tractability statement.\n12. Storage: P1 fp32 snapshot = 4.96 GB (A) / 6.12 GB (B); chosen retention (3 full dumps + full spectra + top-64 vectors at 12 grid points) = 16.31 GB/run (A), 20.23 GB/run (B); P3 inline spectra \u2248 0.02-0.03 GB/run; totals 548 GB main grid + 373 GB CP + 390 GB 7B \u2248 1.31 TB. Basis for the \u00a712 retention scheme and drop order.\n13. Training budget at an assumed 180 TFLOP/s effective: instr-FT (51 M tokens) 0.38-0.47 EFLOP = 0.59-0.72 GPU-h/run; 30 main-grid runs \u2248 19.8 GPU-h; CP (200 M tokens) 15 runs \u2248 34.4 GPU-h; 7B 5 runs \u2248 15.3 GPU-h. Hypothetical run-environment planning context only, with a 2-4\u00d7 wall-clock caveat.\n14. Blocking arithmetic: B = L+1 blocks (A: 17, B: 29); at T \u2248 1560 steps (51 M tokens / (64\u00d7512)) each tensor receives \u2248 92 BAdam updates vs 1560 AdamW updates \u2192 the 17\u00d7 applied-update gap that makes arm 4 mandatory.\n15. Tail-coverage floor in concrete units: 10 % of min-dim 2048 \u2192 \u2265 205 tail points above x_min; min-dim 1536 \u2192 \u2265 154 (rule R2).\n\nD. Researcher directives implemented verbatim: full-parameter primary with LoRA separated into its own verdict table; TOST bands [0.8,1.25] and \u00b10.5 with per-module ratio distributions and in/out-of-band counts by module type plus a kind\u00d7arm interaction override; matched-step primary, mandatory step-thinned AdamW arm, matched-loss secondary with explicit drop note, MP null and SGD negative control retained; R1-R4 including the added checkpoint-flip \u21d2 UNSTABLE counted-not-averaged rule; two ~0.5-1.5B models / \u22653 seeds / instruction + one continued-pretraining condition with a conditional 7B and an explicit drop order (block-granularity ablations before model count); pinned pseudo-code + A1-A9 assumption table + ablations ranked by overturning power; P1 headline vs P3 explanation with the four-cell pre-declared disagreement table; W_0 treated as a first-class confound with the norm-ratio covariate in every table; finite-size restrictions and the storage/sampling scheme carried forward unchanged.", "stage": "result", "uncertainties": ["Every scientific quantity is unknown by construction: whether the perturbation is heavy-tailed, whether that shape is stable across checkpoints, and whether effective rank is equivalent between BAdam and AdamW. The design only fixes how these will be decided.", "Whether the heavy-tail conjunct and the comparability conjunct will hold simultaneously; the design reports the conjunction as YES/NO/INCONCLUSIVE rather than averaging the two.", "Whether P1 and P3 will agree; the four-cell interpretation table in \u00a79 covers both directions of disagreement but cannot predict which occurs.", "Whether the step-thinned AdamW control will absorb the BAdam-vs-AdamW gap, which would reclassify any difference as an update-frequency effect rather than a block-scheduling effect - the design's main risk of a null mechanism claim.", "Exact BAdam blocking parameters (A1-A9) are assumptions with no repo to pin; the ranked ablation ladder exists precisely because the headline's robustness rests on A1, A8, A9 and arm 4.", "Real compute and wall-clock availability, and whether the eventual execution environment resolves the python 3.14 wheel question; the 7B arm and Model C are conditional and may be dropped under the stated order.", "Statistical power at 3 seeds under cluster bootstrap over layers; underpowered cells will be reported as a limitation rather than compensated by loosening the equivalence bands.", "Whether optimizer-state precision differences between block-scheduled and full-model arms can be fully neutralized at 7B scale; if not, that arm is descriptive-only by prior agreement."]}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
